window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("customer-data-evaluation", {"topics":[{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/domain-prompt-engineering","sources":[],"why":"A frontier model has read a great deal about insurance, logistics or clinical coding in general. What it\nhas never seen is the customer's internal policy manual, the exceptions their senior staff apply from\nmemory, or the fact that in this company \"active account\" means \"billed in the last 90 days\" rather than\n\"logged in recently\". Those private definitions are exactly where an AI deployment wins or loses,\nbecause the customer's experts judge outputs against them, not against textbook knowledge.\n\nThe naive approach is a persona prompt: \"You are an expert claims adjuster with 20 years of experience.\"\nIt fails for a predictable reason. A persona changes tone and confidence, but it carries no information\nthe model did not already have, so the model fills gaps with the most common public interpretation. The\noutput sounds expert and is wrong in exactly the places the customer cares about, which is worse than\nsounding uncertain. The second naive approach, pasting the entire 200-page policy manual into the\nprompt, fails differently: manuals are written for humans who already share context, they contradict\nthemselves across versions, and they bury the three rules that decide most cases under fifty that\nrarely matter. Domain prompt engineering sits between the two: extract the decision-relevant knowledge,\nmake it explicit and unambiguous, and prove each piece earns its place on real customer cases.","how":"### 1. Elicit the decision procedure, not the domain\nInterview SMEs around concrete cases, not abstractions. Hand them ten real inputs and ask \"what would you\ndo, and why?\" People cannot list their own rules from memory, but they can explain a decision on a case\nin front of them. Record each reason as a candidate rule along with the case that triggered it, because\nthe case later becomes an eval item that proves the rule works. Ask SMEs to also say *which* reason\ndecided each case; that annotation (\"rule R3 decided this\") is what later lets you tell a precedence\nerror from a definition error.\n\n### 2. Write a glossary of contested terms\nMost domain errors are definitional. List every term whose customer meaning differs from the common one,\ndefine it operationally (what data field, what threshold, what time window), and state the default when\nthe input is ambiguous. A glossary entry like \"Late: delivered after the promised window, where the\nwindow is extended by any customer-requested reschedule\" removes a whole class of disagreements. It is\nwritten this way *because* the model resolves undefined terms toward the public meaning, and says so\nexplicitly (\"use these definitions, not general meanings\") because a definition that merely sits in\nthe prompt competes with a strong prior and can lose.\n\n### 3. Order the rules as a procedure\nDomain policies have precedence: regulatory rules override contract terms, which override house\npreferences. Write rules in the order they should be checked and say explicitly which wins on conflict.\nData-validity checks (\"delivery time missing\") go before any rule that needs that data, because a rule\nevaluated on missing data invites the model to invent the value. An unordered bullet list lets the\nmodel weight rules by how prominent they look, which is not the customer's precedence.\n\n### 4. Choose examples at the boundaries\nFew-shot examples from the customer's own data teach format and judgement at once. Pick them where the\ndecision flips (just inside and just outside a threshold, the exception that overrides the default)\n*because* easy central examples teach nothing the instructions did not already say. Pairs that differ in\none field are the strongest form: they isolate the feature the customer's rule turns on. Keep examples\nout of the golden set, or the eval measures memorisation. Watch the label mix of the examples too,\nbecause models tend to drift toward labels that are over-represented in the shots.\n\n### 5. Specify abstention and escalation\nDomain work always has cases outside policy. Give the model a legitimate exit (\"needs_review\" with the\nreason) and say when to use it. Without one, it is forced to guess, and guesses on out-of-policy cases\nare where customers lose trust fastest.\n\n### 6. Fix the output contract\nState the closed label set and a machine-checkable format, and ask the output to name the rule that\ndecided it and the glossary terms it relied on. The citation is not proof of the model's reasoning (a\nself-reported rule can be a post-hoc rationalisation), but it turns failures into a diagnosable signal:\na wrong label that cites the wrong rule points at precedence or rule wording; a wrong label that cites\nthe right rule points at a term definition.\n\n### 7. Separate stable knowledge from per-request context\nPolicies and glossary go in a versioned system prompt; customer-specific facts that change per request\n(the account, the contract, the retrieved clause) go in the user turn or via retrieval. When the domain\nknowledge outgrows a prompt, move the long tail into retrieval and keep only the core procedure inline.\nThe split also matters for cost: a stable prefix can be served from a prompt cache where the provider\nsupports it, while per-request content cannot.\n\n### 8. Test every change, on a split you did not tune on\nEach rule, glossary entry and example is a hypothesis. The cases SMEs used to elicit the rules are, by\nconstruction, the cases the prompt fits best, so iterate on an iteration split and keep a holdout that\nnobody reads while writing rules. After each change, run the iteration split and review failures with\nSMEs; at milestones, run the holdout. A rule that moves no case is redundant or untested (ablate it: drop\nit and re-run); a rule that fixes three cases and breaks two needs rewording. If the iteration score\nclimbs while the holdout stays flat, the rules are memorising cases rather than capturing policy.\nVersion the prompt per customer so a regression can be traced to the change that caused it.","example":"A freight customer wants shipment exceptions classified. The prompt is assembled from SME-owned parts so\neach piece can be versioned and tested separately, and the output cites the rule that fired so failures\ncan be sorted into precedence errors and definition errors.\n\n```python\nimport json\nGLOSSARY = {\n    \"late\": \"Delivered after the promised window. The window moves only if the consignee requested a \"\n            \"reschedule (reschedule_by == 'consignee'). Carrier reschedules do NOT move it.\",\n    \"damaged\": \"Consignee noted damage on the POD, or a photo claim was filed within 48h of delivery.\",\n}\nRULES = [  # checked in order; the first matching rule wins\n    (\"R1\", \"hazmat flag set and any exception exists -> needs_review (regulatory)\"),\n    (\"R2\", \"delivery_ts missing -> needs_review; never infer a delivery time\"),\n    (\"R3\", \"both late and damaged apply -> damaged (claims outrank SLA credits)\"),\n    (\"R4\", \"otherwise apply the definitions -> late, damaged or on_time\"),\n]\nEXAMPLES = [  # SME boundary pair, differs only in who rescheduled; excluded from the golden set\n    ({\"promised_end\": \"10:00\", \"delivery_ts\": \"10:20\", \"reschedule_by\": \"consignee\",\n      \"new_end\": \"12:00\"}, {\"label\": \"on_time\", \"rule\": \"R4\"}),\n    ({\"promised_end\": \"10:00\", \"delivery_ts\": \"10:20\", \"reschedule_by\": \"carrier\",\n      \"new_end\": \"12:00\"}, {\"label\": \"late\", \"rule\": \"R4\"}),\n]\n\ndef build_prompt(version=\"acme-freight-v7\"):\n    terms = \"\\n\".join(f\"- {k}: {v}\" for k, v in GLOSSARY.items())\n    rules = \"\\n\".join(f\"{rid}. {text}\" for rid, text in RULES)\n    shots = \"\\n\".join(f\"Input: {json.dumps(x)}\\nOutput: {json.dumps(y)}\" for x, y in EXAMPLES)\n    return (f\"[prompt {version}]\\nClassify the shipment. Use these definitions, not general \"\n            f\"meanings:\\n{terms}\\nRules, in priority order:\\n{rules}\\n\"\n            'Reply only with JSON: {\"label\": on_time|late|damaged|needs_review, \"rule\": \"R1\"..\"R4\"}\\n'\n            f\"Examples:\\n{shots}\")\n\ndef diagnose(results):\n    \"\"\"results: dicts with the SME's gold_label and gold_rule, and the model's label and rule.\"\"\"\n    out = {\"correct\": 0, \"precedence\": 0, \"definition\": 0}\n    for r in results:\n        if r[\"label\"] == r[\"gold_label\"]:\n            out[\"correct\"] += 1\n        elif r[\"rule\"] != r[\"gold_rule\"]:\n            out[\"precedence\"] += 1      # fired the wrong rule: fix ordering or rule wording\n        else:\n            out[\"definition\"] += 1      # right rule, wrong reading of a term: fix the glossary\n    return out\n```\n\nA walk-through of one iteration, numbers illustrative. Prompt v6 defined \"late\" as \"after the promised\nwindow\" with no reschedule clause. On a 160-case iteration split it gets 121 right; `diagnose` reports 9\nprecedence and 30 definition errors, and 24 of the definition errors are carrier reschedules the model\ntreated as moving the window, which is the public reading of \"rescheduled delivery\". The SMEs confirm\nthe rule, v7 adds the consignee-only clause and the boundary pair above, and the iteration split rises\nto 146 of 160. The holdout of 80 cases, untouched while writing the rule, goes from 60 to 71, so the fix\ngeneralises rather than memorising the 24 cases. The 9 precedence errors are a separate problem: most\nare damaged-and-late shipments labelled late, so the next change targets R3's wording, not the glossary.","tradeoffs":["Explicit rules make behaviour predictable and auditable, but each rule adds tokens and a chance of conflict; once rules number in the dozens, retrieval of the relevant subset usually beats inlining all.","Customer-specific few-shot examples teach judgement fast, but they leak format quirks and can bias the label distribution; when examples skew toward one class, predictions skew with them.","A per-customer prompt fits each client precisely, but multiplies maintenance; once three customers share most rules, factor a shared base prompt with customer overlays.","Abstention (\"needs_review\") protects trust on out-of-policy cases, but raises the human workload; if the review queue exceeds what operations can staff, tighten the abstention criteria with SMEs."],"pitfalls":["Writing rules from the policy manual instead of from cases; the symptom is a long prompt that scores no better than the short one, because the manual rules were not the ones deciding outcomes.","Leaving a contested term undefined; the symptom is SMEs and model disagreeing on the same subset of cases over and over, all of which contain that word.","Reusing few-shot examples inside the golden set; the symptom is an eval score that drops sharply when a fresh sample of production cases is labelled.","Tuning rules on the same cases used to elicit them, with no holdout; the symptom is an iteration score that keeps rising while a fresh sample or the holdout does not move.","Trusting the model's cited rule as an explanation of its reasoning; the symptom is \"fixing\" a rule the output cited and seeing no change, because the citation was rationalised after the label.","Encoding one SME's personal preference as policy; the symptom is a second SME rejecting outputs the first one approved, which means the rule needs the policy owner's decision, not more prompting."],"when":"Reach for domain prompt engineering whenever a general model's outputs are fluent but judged wrong by the\ncustomer's experts, and the errors cluster around definitions, precedence or exceptions. It is the first\nlever before retrieval or fine-tuning because it is cheap, reversible and inspectable. Move on when the\nneeded knowledge is too large or changes too often for a prompt (use retrieval), or when the behaviour\nis a style or skill that instructions cannot pin down after several rounds (consider fine-tuning).\nMeasure with the customer's golden set and per-term failure counts. In interviews, show that you treat\nthe prompt as a tested artefact owned jointly with SMEs, not as clever phrasing.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds prompts from versioned glossary, rule and example components, and wires each change to a golden-set eval run with per-term failure breakdowns.","architect":"Decides the boundary between shared base prompt, per-customer overlays and retrieved policy, so domain knowledge scales across clients without copy-paste drift.","fde":"Runs case-based interviews with the customer's experts, extracts definitions and precedence, and gets the policy owner to resolve rules where SMEs disagree."},"check":{"q":"Your insurance-claims classifier scores well on public examples but the customer's adjusters reject\na third of its outputs. Adding \"You are a senior adjuster\" did nothing. What do you do next, and why?","a":"The persona carried no new information, so the model is still applying public definitions. I would\npull the rejected outputs, sit with two adjusters and ask them to explain each rejection on the case.\nI expect clusters around a few terms or exceptions, such as what counts as a pre-existing condition. I\nwould write operational definitions for those terms, order the rules by the customer's precedence, add\nboundary examples from their data that are kept out of the golden set, and add a needs_review exit for\nout-of-policy cases, and have outputs cite the rule applied so remaining failures split into precedence\nand definition errors. Then I would re-run the iteration split, check the rejected clusters shrink without\nnew failures elsewhere, and confirm the gain on a holdout the rules were not written from. Where adjusters\ndisagree with each other, I escalate to the policy owner rather than prompting around it."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a persona prompt (\"you are an expert in X\") rarely fix domain errors on customer data?","options":["Models ignore system prompts that describe a role","It changes tone and confidence but adds none of the customer's private definitions or policies","Personas only work with fine-tuned models","It makes the prompt too long for the context window"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The errors come from missing information: the customer's own meanings and precedence rules. A persona adds no facts, so the model still falls back to public interpretations, only more confidently."},{"q":"Where should few-shot examples for a domain prompt come from?","options":["Random central cases from the golden set, so the prompt and the eval agree","Synthetic examples written by the engineer to avoid exposing customer data","Boundary cases from the customer's data where the decision flips, kept out of the golden set","The longest and most complex cases, since easy cases need no help"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Boundary examples teach where the customer's rule diverges from the default. Taking them from the golden set would make the eval measure memorisation instead of generalisation."},{"q":"Two SMEs consistently disagree about whether a case is \"late\". What is the right fix?","options":["Add more few-shot examples until the model matches the more senior SME","Average their labels in the golden set","Remove the cases from the eval because they are ambiguous","Escalate to the policy owner for an operational definition, then encode it in the glossary"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Persistent SME disagreement means the policy itself is undefined. Prompting cannot resolve it; only the person who owns the policy can, and the decision then becomes a glossary entry and eval cases."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/golden-dataset-creation-with-client-smes","sources":[],"why":"In a customer deployment the question that decides the project is \"do your outputs meet our standard?\"\nOnly the customer's experts can define that standard, and they define it case by case. If the vendor\nwrites the reference answers, the eval measures agreement with the vendor's guesses, and the first time\nan SME reviews production output the score is revealed as fiction. The general idea of a golden dataset\nis covered in the evals module; this lesson is about the engagement mechanics of building one inside a\nclient.\n\nThree things break without a deliberate process. First, SME time is the scarcest resource on the\nproject: senior adjusters, clinicians or lawyers are billing or seeing patients, so an ad-hoc request\nfor \"some labels\" produces a few dozen easy cases and then silence. Second, experts disagree more than\nanyone expects, and an unmanaged disagreement silently becomes label noise that caps every metric.\nThird, the data itself is governed: it may contain personal or regulated information that cannot leave\nthe customer's environment, so \"export a CSV and label it in our tool\" may be forbidden. The naive path,\nengineers labelling from the documentation and asking SMEs to \"spot check\", fails on all three, and it\nleaves no artefact the customer will sign as the acceptance standard.","how":"### 1. Anchor the dataset to a decision\nWrite down what the dataset will be used to decide: prompt iteration, model choice, go-live acceptance.\nThis sets the size, the error tolerance and who must sign off. An acceptance dataset needs the business\nowner's signature; an iteration dataset only needs the working SMEs. Size follows from the precision the\ndecision needs, per segment that has its own threshold: for a pass rate near p and a desired 95%\nhalf-width E, n ≈ 1.96² · p(1−p) / E². At p = 0.9 and E = 0.05 that is about 140 items, and it applies\nto *each* segment with its own bar, which is why segment thresholds, not the headline, usually drive the\ntotal and the expert-hours.\n\n### 2. Clear data access first\nAgree where the data lives, who may see it, and whether de-identification is needed before anyone\nlabels. Labelling tools often must run inside the customer's environment. Doing this first matters\n*because* discovering a governance blocker after SMEs have labelled 200 exported records means throwing\nthe work away.\n\n### 3. Sample from production, stratified\nDraw candidate inputs from real recent traffic, stratified by the segments the business cares about,\nwith deliberate oversampling of rare high-stakes cases (see representative test-case sampling).\nSynthetic or hand-picked items are allowed only as a tagged minority, *because* experts write\ncleaner inputs than the world does.\n\n### 4. Write guidelines, then calibrate\nDraft annotation guidelines with one or two SMEs, run a calibration batch that several experts label\nindependently, measure agreement, and revise the guideline where they split. Only then scale up.\nCalibrating first is cheap; discovering disagreement after 500 labels is not.\n\n### 5. Label for reference and rationale\nEach item stores more than an answer, because each field answers a later question:\n\n| Field | Why it is there |\n|---|---|\n| input, source record id | reproducibility, and deletion if the customer revokes the record |\n| reference answer, or acceptance criteria | closed tasks get a label; open-ended ones get must-include facts and must-not errors, because many wordings are correct |\n| rationale citing the guideline section | lets an engineer tell a model error from a label error later |\n| ambiguous / difficulty flag | ambiguous items are scored separately, not silently counted |\n| labeller, guideline version, date | a definition change can then be traced to the labels it affects |\n\nDecide early whether SMEs label from scratch or correct a pre-label (model output or engineer draft).\nPre-labels can cut expert time substantially but anchor reviewers toward the draft, so measure how\noften SMEs change them; a change rate near zero on hard items signals rubber-stamping.\n\n### 6. Double-label a subset and adjudicate\nHave a fixed fraction labelled by two experts, compute chance-corrected agreement (Cohen's κ) per\ncategory, and send disagreements to an adjudicator, normally the policy owner. Persistent disagreement\nin a category is a policy gap; resolve it as a written rule rather than by majority vote, *because* the\nmodel will be judged against the rule in production. Agreement also sets a ceiling on what the metric\ncan show: if two experts agree on 85% of items, a model \"scoring\" 95% against one of them is partly\nfitting that expert's quirks.\n\n### 7. Freeze, version and sign off\nFreeze a version with a changelog, record guideline version and labeller per item, split off a holdout\n(stratified, so rare segments appear in it) that the team never inspects during iteration, and get\nwritten sign-off from the business owner that this set, at this threshold, is the acceptance standard.\n\n### 8. Refresh on a schedule\nProduction drifts. Add newly sampled cases each cycle, retire items that no longer reflect policy, and\nnever edit a label silently; changes go through the same review and bump the version.","example":"A worked plan for a commercial-insurance claims triage pilot, with numbers stated as illustrative.\n\nThe customer offers three senior adjusters for two hours a week each, six expert-hours weekly. The\ntarget is a 400-item set. Week 1: the data team confirms claims can be labelled only inside the\ncustomer's tenant, so the labelling tool is deployed there and policyholder names are masked. A\nstratified sample of 600 candidates is drawn from the last quarter: by line of business, claim size\nband, and an oversample of the roughly 3% of claims with litigation flags so they get 40 of the 400\nfinal slots rather than the 12 a proportional sample would give.\n\nWeek 2: a 30-item calibration batch is labelled by all three adjusters independently; at an assumed 4\nminutes per item that is 90 labels, about 6 hours, the whole week's budget. Agreement is high on routing\nto fast-track, but they split on \"potential subrogation\" in 8 of 30 cases. The claims director rules\nthat subrogation applies only when a third party is named in the first notice, and that rule goes into\nthe guideline and the prompt.\n\nWeeks 3 to 8: 400 items single-labelled take about 27 hours; 20% double-labelled adds about 5, so\nroughly 32 expert-hours, between five and six weeks of the offered time. The FDE shows this to the\nsponsor up front rather than promising three weeks. Adjudication takes one 45-minute session per week.\n\nWeek 9: version 1.0 is frozen and split 240 iteration / 160 holdout, stratified, so 16 litigation items\nland in the holdout. The FDE then runs the interval arithmetic in front of the sponsor before anyone\nsigns. On 160 holdout items, 90% overall routing accuracy carries about ±5 points (√(0.9·0.1/160) ≈\n0.024). On 16 litigation items, an 85% floor carries about ±17 points, which cannot separate a good\nsystem from a poor one. So the signed bar is 90% overall on the holdout, and 85% on litigation measured\non those 16 plus 60 litigation claims sampled and labelled after iteration ends (about 4 more\nexpert-hours), giving roughly ±8 points on 76 items with no tuned-on item counted.","tradeoffs":["More SMEs per item gives cleaner labels and measurable agreement, but multiplies scarce expert time; double-label a subset, and label everything twice only when errors are very costly.","Using the customer's labelling environment satisfies governance, but you lose your tooling and automation; when data can be de-identified safely, an external tool may be faster and still compliant.","A larger set narrows confidence intervals, but every extra item costs expert minutes; once intervals are tighter than the decision needs, spend the time on rare strata instead.","Freezing early gives a stable target, but freezes early mistakes too; keep a short revision window after the first full eval run before declaring the acceptance version."],"pitfalls":["Engineers writing reference answers and SMEs only spot-checking; the symptom is a high eval score followed by SMEs rejecting a large share of production outputs.","Letting the most available SME label everything; the symptom is a second expert disagreeing with a third of the labels when brought in late.","Resolving disagreement by majority vote; the symptom is the same category flip-flopping between dataset versions because no policy was ever written.","Reusing golden items as prompt examples or retrieval content; the symptom is a score that collapses on freshly sampled production cases.","Signing a segment threshold the holdout is too small to test; the symptom is a go/no-go meeting where a 3-item swing in a 16-item segment decides the outcome.","Skipping the formal sign-off; the symptom is the acceptance bar being renegotiated after results come in."],"when":"Build a client-SME golden dataset for any deployment where the customer's experts, not a public\nbenchmark, decide whether outputs are acceptable, which is nearly every enterprise deployment. Start in\nthe first two weeks of a pilot, in parallel with the first prototype, because SME scheduling and data\naccess are the long poles. A small iteration set from the vendor team is fine for day-one debugging, but\nit must never be the acceptance standard. Measure agreement on the double-labelled subset, coverage per\nstratum, and label churn between versions. In interviews, emphasise sign-off, governance and policy\nresolution, not just \"collect examples\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the sampling and labelling pipeline, stores labeller, guideline version and rationale per item, and keeps the holdout physically separate from iteration data.","architect":"Decides where labelling runs given data residency and access constraints, and how datasets are versioned per customer so they feed regression suites later.","fde":"Negotiates SME time with the business owner, facilitates calibration, routes disagreements to the policy owner, and secures written sign-off on the acceptance set."},"check":{"q":"The customer offers one SME for one hour a week and wants go-live in six weeks. What do you do?","a":"I would show the arithmetic: at a few minutes per item, one hour a week yields at most about\nninety labels by go-live, single-labelled, with no agreement estimate, which cannot support an\nacceptance decision with useful confidence. Then I would offer options: add a second SME for the\ncalibration and double-labelled subset; have the vendor team pre-label so SMEs only review and correct,\nwhile measuring how often they change the pre-label to detect anchoring; narrow the scope to the\nhighest-volume segments; or move the date. I would also ask the business owner to sign off on the\nreduced confidence explicitly, because the risk is theirs to accept."},"quiz":[{"q":"Two adjusters disagree on a category in a quarter of calibration cases. What should happen?","options":["Take the majority label once a third adjuster votes","Escalate to the policy owner, write the ruling into the guideline, and relabel the affected cases","Drop the category from the dataset because it is ambiguous","Keep both labels and score the model correct if it matches either"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A consistent split signals a policy gap. Voting hides it and it resurfaces in production; a written ruling from the owner fixes labels, guideline and prompt together."},{"q":"Why should data-access and governance questions be settled before labelling starts?","options":["Labelling tools cannot read customer data formats","Governance reviews always take longer than labelling","SMEs refuse to label without a signed contract","If labelled data later turns out to be unusable outside the customer's environment, the labelling work is lost"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The expensive asset is expert time. A late governance blocker can invalidate labels done in the wrong place, so where data lives and who may see it must be agreed first."},{"q":"What makes a client golden dataset \"authoritative\" for go-live?","options":["It has more than a thousand items","It was labelled by the vendor's most experienced engineers","The customer's business owner signed off on the set and the threshold as the acceptance standard","It matches the distribution of a public benchmark"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Authority comes from the customer's agreement, not size or vendor expertise. Without sign-off, the bar tends to be renegotiated after results are seen."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/representative-test-case-sampling","sources":[],"why":"An eval score is an estimate of how the system will behave on future traffic, and an estimate is only as\ngood as the sample behind it. The customer usually hands over one of two things: the cases their team\nremembers (the weird ones, the ones that caused an escalation, the demo favourites) or a random export.\nBoth mislead, in opposite directions.\n\nHand-picked sets over-represent memorable edge cases and under-represent the boring bulk, so the score\nis pessimistic on volume and says nothing reliable about the mix. Worse, the cases people remember are\nthe ones already discussed, so they are biased toward problems the team has seen. A simple random sample\nfixes the mix but fails on rare strata: if legal-threat tickets are 2% of traffic, a 300-item random\nsample contains about six of them, too few to tell 60% accuracy from 90%, even though a wrong answer\nthere is the most expensive kind. There is also a time problem: a sample drawn from last year's traffic\nmisses new products, policy changes and seasonal patterns. Representative sampling exists to produce an\neval set that is both faithful to production in aggregate and dense enough in the strata where decisions\nare made.","how":"### 1. Define the population and the sampling unit\nState exactly which traffic the system will see: which channels, products, languages and date range.\nPull from the most recent representative window, ideally the same period of the business cycle the\nlaunch will face, *because* traffic composition drifts and old samples encode old policies. Pick the\nunit deliberately: sample conversations or documents, not turns or pages, when the system is judged on\nthe whole, and cap items per account so one heavy customer cannot dominate. Check what the logs cannot\ncontain, too: if today's process routes legal threats straight to a phone team, they never reach the\nticket log, and no sampling scheme recovers them; that gap must be filled from another source.\n\n### 2. Choose strata from the decision, not the data\nStrata are the segments where you need a separate answer: intent or document type, customer tier,\nlanguage, risk class, input length band, source system. Pick them by asking \"if quality differed here,\nwould we act differently?\" Each stratum you add costs sample budget, so keep only those with a\nconsequence. When nobody knows the segments yet, cluster a large unlabelled slice by embedding,\nhave SMEs name the clusters, and promote the ones with distinct risk to strata.\n\n### 3. Size the budget and allocate with a floor\nPrecision depends on the count in a stratum, not its share of traffic. For a pass rate near p measured\non n items, the standard error is about √(p(1−p)/n): at p = 0.9 and n = 100 that is 0.03, so a 95%\ninterval is roughly ±6 points, and six items cannot distinguish anything. Inverting it gives the count\na stratum needs for a target half-width E: n ≈ 1.96² · p(1−p) / E². When failures should be rare, the\n\"rule of three\" is the quick check: zero failures in n items still leaves a one-sided 95% upper bound of\nabout 3/n on the failure rate, so proving \"under 1% failures\" needs roughly 300 clean items in that\nstratum.\n\nAllocation is then a deliberate compromise. Proportional allocation mirrors traffic and is best for the\nheadline number only when strata behave alike; Neyman allocation (n_h ∝ N_h·σ_h, where σ_h is the\nstratum's spread) minimises the variance of the population estimate. Neither guarantees a usable count\nin a 2% stratum, so give every important stratum a floor first and distribute the rest of the budget in\nproportion to traffic.\n\n### 4. Deduplicate before sampling\nProduction logs are full of near-duplicates: templated emails, retries, the same invoice resubmitted.\nCluster by normalised text or embedding similarity and sample clusters, not rows, *because* fifty copies\nof one template make the set look large while testing one case.\n\n### 5. Re-weight to report the population number\nBecause strata were sampled at different rates, the raw average over the set is biased toward the\noversampled strata. With W_h the stratum's share of traffic and p_h its measured pass rate, the\nproduction estimate is p̂ = Σ W_h·p_h, and its standard error is √(Σ W_h²·p_h(1−p_h)/n_h). The second\nformula shows the price of floors: oversampled small strata contribute almost nothing to the variance\n(W_h² is tiny), while the bulk stratum, which lost items to the floors, contributes most of it. Report\nper-stratum numbers with their own intervals alongside the weighted one.\n\n### 6. Add targeted cases as a labelled minority\nKnown failure modes, adversarial inputs and SME-nominated edge cases belong in the suite, but tagged as a\nseparate \"targeted\" stratum that is excluded from the weighted population metric. Mixing them in\nsilently makes the headline number unrepresentative.\n\n### 7. Keep it fresh and leak-free\nResample a slice each cycle from new traffic, keep a holdout nobody tunes against, and make sure no\nsampled case also appears as a prompt example or in a fine-tuning set.","example":"Ticket triage for a telecom customer. Of 10,000 recent tickets, 9,000 are billing, 800 cancellation and\n200 legal threats (illustrative mix). The budget is 300 SME-labelled items, with a floor of 60 per\nstratum so the rare one is measurable.\n\n```python\nimport random, math\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef stratified_sample(logs, key, budget, floor, seed=7):\n    \"\"\"Floor per stratum first, the rest of the budget proportionally. Returns sample, weights.\"\"\"\n    rng, strata = random.Random(seed), defaultdict(list)\n    for r in logs:\n        strata[key(r)].append(r)\n    spare = budget - floor * len(strata)\n    sample, weight = [], {}\n    for s, items in strata.items():\n        n = min(len(items), floor + round(spare * len(items) / len(logs)))\n        sample += [(s, r) for r in rng.sample(items, n)]\n        weight[s] = len(items) / n          # each sampled item stands for this many real ones\n    return sample, weight\n\ndef weighted_estimate(graded, weight, N):\n    \"\"\"graded: (stratum, correct) pairs. Population estimate and its standard error.\"\"\"\n    by = defaultdict(list)\n    for s, ok in graded:\n        by[s].append(ok)\n    est = se2 = 0.0\n    for s, oks in by.items():\n        W, p = weight[s] * len(oks) / N, sum(oks) / len(oks)   # W = stratum share of traffic\n        est += W * p\n        se2 += W * W * p * (1 - p) / len(oks)\n    return est, math.sqrt(se2)\n\nlogs = ([{\"intent\": \"billing\"}] * 9000 + [{\"intent\": \"cancel\"}] * 800\n        + [{\"intent\": \"legal\"}] * 200)\nsample, w = stratified_sample(logs, key=lambda r: r[\"intent\"], budget=300, floor=60)\nn = {s: sum(t == s for t, _ in sample) for s in w}\nprint(n)            # {'billing': 168, 'cancel': 70, 'legal': 62}\n\ntrue_acc = {\"billing\": 0.95, \"cancel\": 0.85, \"legal\": 0.60}   # pretend grading results\ngraded = [(s, i < round(true_acc[s] * n[s])) for s in n for i in range(n[s])]\nraw = sum(ok for _, ok in graded) / len(graded)\nest, se = weighted_estimate(graded, w, len(logs))\nprint(f\"unweighted {raw:.3f}  weighted {est:.3f} ± {1.96 * se:.3f}\")\n# unweighted 0.857  weighted 0.938 ± 0.030\n```\n\nThe unweighted 85.7% understates production quality because legal threats are 21% of the set but 2% of\ntraffic; the weighted 93.8% is the population estimate. The floors cost little on the headline: ±3.0\npoints against about ±2.7 for a simple random sample of 300 at the same accuracy, because the legal and\ncancel strata carry tiny weights. What they buy is the per-stratum view, which is the actionable one: at\n62 items, legal accuracy near 60% carries roughly ±12 points of uncertainty, enough to know it is far\nbelow billing and must be fixed before launch. A proportional 300-item sample would have held about six\nlegal tickets.","tradeoffs":["Floors make rare strata measurable, but they spend budget away from the bulk, widening the interval on the population metric; when the overall number is the acceptance criterion, lower the floors.","More strata give finer diagnosis, but each one needs its own floor; beyond a handful, merge strata whose quality you would act on identically.","Recent-window sampling tracks current traffic, but can miss seasonal or quarter-end patterns; when the business is seasonal, sample across the cycle or add the missing period explicitly.","Cluster-level deduplication tests more distinct behaviours per label, but underweights genuinely high-volume templates; weight clusters by size when reporting the population metric."],"pitfalls":["Reporting the raw average of an oversampled set as \"accuracy\"; the symptom is a production metric that is consistently higher or lower than the eval said, by a stable amount.","Letting the customer hand-pick the eval set; the symptom is a set dominated by escalation stories and a score that does not move when the bulk of traffic improves.","Sampling before deduplicating; the symptom is a large share of the set sharing one template, and fixes for that template swinging the score far more than their production impact.","Mixing targeted adversarial cases into the population metric; the symptom is a headline number that drops every time someone adds a new red-team case.","Treating the log as the population when part of the traffic bypasses it; the symptom is post-launch complaints about a case type that never appeared in the eval set at all.","Sampling from a period before a policy or product change; the symptom is SMEs relabelling many items as \"no longer how we do it\"."],"when":"Use stratified, re-weighted sampling whenever an eval set will inform a ship decision or be quoted to a\ncustomer, and whenever traffic contains rare segments with high cost of error. Simple random sampling is\nfine for a quick health check on homogeneous traffic. Decide strata with the business owner at the same\ntime as acceptance criteria, because they define where thresholds apply. Track per-stratum counts,\ninterval widths and the gap between eval and observed production metrics. In interviews, being able to\nexplain why a random sample fails for a 2% stratum, and how to re-weight, is the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements deduplication, stratified draws with floors and population re-weighting, and reports per-stratum results with confidence intervals rather than one average.","architect":"Defines the sampling frame and refresh cadence as part of the eval platform, so every customer's suite is drawn and weighted the same auditable way.","fde":"Works with the customer to name the strata that matter commercially, and explains why their hand-picked favourites become a targeted stratum, not the whole set."},"check":{"q":"A customer's eval set shows 92% accuracy, but production QA reports 81%. The set was built from\ncases their team nominated. What is likely happening and how do you fix it?","a":"Nominated sets reflect memory, not traffic. The nominated cases are probably heavy in categories the\nteam already worked on and light in the high-volume segments where production errors actually occur,\nor drawn from an older period. I would compare the strata mix of the set against recent traffic, then\ndraw a fresh stratified sample from the latest production window, deduplicated, with floors for\nhigh-stakes segments, and re-weight results to the production mix. I would keep the nominated cases as a\ntargeted stratum outside the headline number. If the new weighted metric is close to 81%, the gap was\nsampling; if not, I would look at grading differences between eval and production QA."},"quiz":[{"q":"Legal-threat tickets are 2% of traffic. Why is a 300-item simple random sample a poor eval for them?","options":["Random sampling systematically excludes rare categories","Legal tickets need a different metric than accuracy","It contains only about six such tickets, too few to estimate their accuracy with useful precision","Rare categories should be evaluated only in production"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Precision depends on the number of items in the stratum. Six items give an interval tens of points wide, so a floor (oversampling) is needed, followed by re-weighting for the overall metric."},{"q":"After oversampling rare strata, how should you report overall production accuracy?","options":["Use the plain average over all sampled items","Report only the worst stratum","Drop the oversampled items and average the rest","Weight each stratum's results by its share of production traffic"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Oversampling distorts the raw average toward rare strata. Weighting by stratum size over sample count recovers the population estimate while keeping the per-stratum precision you paid for."},{"q":"Why deduplicate production logs before sampling test cases?","options":["Duplicates break most labelling tools","Near-duplicate templates inflate the set while testing one behaviour, so fixes to them distort the score","Duplicates contain more personal data than unique records","Deduplication guarantees the set matches production traffic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fifty copies of a templated email are one test case wearing fifty labels. Sampling clusters gives more distinct behaviours per expert label; re-weighting by cluster size restores the population view."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/sme-review-and-annotation-guidelines","sources":[],"why":"When you ask three senior experts \"is this answer good?\", you get three answers shaped by three private\nstandards. One fails an answer for a missing caveat, one for a slightly wrong tone, one passes anything\nfactually correct. Each is a reasonable expert; together they produce labels whose disagreement is\nindistinguishable from model error. If the eval set is labelled this way, a prompt change can move the\nscore just by changing which reviewer saw which items.\n\nThe naive approach is to trust expertise: \"they are the SMEs, they know what good looks like\". Expertise\ntells you what an individual expert believes, but an eval needs a *shared* standard that can be applied\nconsistently, audited later, taught to a new reviewer and eventually encoded into an LLM judge. The\nsecond naive approach is a one-line rubric (\"rate 1 to 5 for quality\"), which invites each reviewer to\nfill in their own meaning of 3 versus 4. Guidelines exist to turn tacit, individual standards into an\nexplicit, shared one; review protocol exists to prevent the process itself (seeing which model produced\nan output, reviewing in a fixed order, discussing before labelling) from biasing the result.","how":"### Two jobs, one guideline\nSMEs do two different things with customer data. **Annotation** creates the reference: the expert sees\nonly the input and writes the correct label or answer. **Review** judges a system's output against the\ninput (and sometimes a reference). Review is faster per item, because recognising an error is easier\nthan producing the answer, but it is anchored by what the system wrote: a plausible output pulls the\nreviewer toward accepting it. Annotation is slower and unanchored. Use annotation for the golden set's\nreferences and review for judging runs, and write one guideline that both follow, so \"correct\" means the\nsame thing in the reference and in the verdict.\n\n### What goes in the guideline\n1. **Scope.** What the reviewer is judging and, just as important, what they are not: \"judge whether the\n   answer is correct for this policy; do not judge writing style unless it misleads\".\n2. **Label set.** Prefer binary or small categorical labels per criterion over a single 1–5 score,\n   *because* narrow questions get consistent answers and broad scales hide which criterion failed.\n3. **Operational definitions.** For each label, the observable conditions: \"Fail on completeness if the\n   answer omits an eligibility condition stated in the source policy\".\n4. **Decision order.** Which criteria to check first and which are disqualifying, so reviewers do not\n   weigh a tone problem against a factual error differently.\n5. **Boundary examples.** Pairs of real cases just either side of each line, with the reason.\n6. **Unsure and out-of-scope paths.** A legitimate \"cannot judge\" label with a reason field, *because*\n   forcing a choice on ambiguous items turns policy gaps into silent noise.\n7. **Rationale requirement.** A short free-text reason on every fail, citing the guideline section.\n\n### The review protocol\n- **Blind.** Hide which system, prompt version or model produced the output, and mix in reference\n  answers, *because* reviewers who know which is the \"new\" version rate it differently.\n- **Randomised order.** Shuffle per reviewer, so fatigue and anchoring effects spread evenly.\n- **Independent first, discuss second.** Labels are committed before any group discussion; otherwise\n  the most senior voice becomes the label.\n- **Measure agreement.** On a double-labelled subset, compute raw agreement and a chance-corrected\n  statistic such as Cohen's κ = (p_obs − p_chance)/(1 − p_chance), where p_chance is the agreement two\n  reviewers would reach by labelling at random with their own label rates. Raw agreement alone is\n  inflated when one label dominates. Because κ itself drops when one label is very rare even if\n  reviewers are good, also report agreement specific to the label you care about: for fails,\n  2·n_both_fail / (2·n_both_fail + n_disagree). For more than two reviewers use Fleiss' κ; when items receive\n  different numbers of labels or the scale is ordinal, Krippendorff's α handles both.\n- **Attribute disagreement.** Use the cited guideline sections to see which criterion causes splits; fix\n  that section, not the reviewers.\n- **Version the guideline.** Store guideline version per label, because a definition change can\n  invalidate earlier labels in that category.\n\n### When to stop iterating\nIterate calibration rounds until agreement is stable and adequate for the decision the labels support.\nThere is no universal threshold; κ bands such as Landis and Koch's are conventions, and a high-stakes\nacceptance set warrants more agreement than an exploratory error analysis.","example":"A benefits-policy assistant for an HR customer. Two HR SMEs review 50 answers independently against\nguideline v0.3, labelling pass or fail and citing the section behind each fail.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef cohen_kappa(a, b):\n    \"\"\"a, b: equal-length label lists from two reviewers on the same items.\"\"\"\n    n = len(a)\n    p_obs = sum(x == y for x, y in zip(a, b)) / n\n    ca, cb = Counter(a), Counter(b)\n    p_chance = sum(ca[k] * cb[k] for k in ca) / (n * n)\n    return (p_obs - p_chance) / (1 - p_chance), p_obs\n\n# 30 both pass, 10 both fail, 6 A-pass/B-fail, 4 A-fail/B-pass (illustrative)\na = [\"pass\"] * 30 + [\"fail\"] * 10 + [\"pass\"] * 6 + [\"fail\"] * 4\nb = [\"pass\"] * 30 + [\"fail\"] * 10 + [\"fail\"] * 6 + [\"pass\"] * 4\n# Guideline section cited by whichever reviewer failed the item, on the 10 disagreements\ncited = [\"G3-completeness\"] * 7 + [\"G1-accuracy\"] * 2 + [\"G5-tone\"]\n\nk, po = cohen_kappa(a, b)\nprint(f\"raw agreement {po:.2f}, kappa {k:.2f}\")   # raw agreement 0.80, kappa 0.52\nprint(Counter(cited).most_common())              # G3 drives most disagreement\nboth_fail, disagree = 10, 10\nprint(f\"agreement on fails {2 * both_fail / (2 * both_fail + disagree):.2f}\")  # 0.67\n```\n\nRaw agreement of 80% looks fine, but chance agreement here is about 58% because both reviewers pass most\nanswers, so κ is only about 0.52. Fail-specific agreement is 0.67: when either reviewer fails an\nanswer, the other agrees only two times in three, and the fails are what the eval exists to find. Seven\nof ten disagreements cite completeness: one SME fails answers\nthat omit the waiting-period rule even when the question did not ask about it. The fix is in G3 (\"an\nanswer is incomplete only if it omits a condition that changes the answer to the question asked\"), plus\ntwo boundary examples, then a fresh calibration batch, not more reviewer training.","tradeoffs":["Binary per-criterion labels give higher agreement and clear diagnosis, but lose gradations of quality; when choosing between two good systems, add pairwise preference judgements alongside them.","Detailed guidelines raise consistency, but long documents go unread; past a few pages, move rarely needed detail into an appendix and keep a one-page decision card for reviewers.","Review of system outputs costs less expert time than annotating from scratch, but anchors on the output; for golden references and hard strata, annotate unaided, and keep review for judging runs.","Blind review removes version bias, but hides context reviewers sometimes need, such as which data source a system had; provide the input context, never the system identity.","An \"unsure\" label surfaces policy gaps honestly, but some reviewers overuse it; when its rate climbs, review those items in session and add boundary examples."],"pitfalls":["Building references by having SMEs approve system outputs; the symptom is a golden set that agrees suspiciously well with the system that drafted it and scores a different system unfairly low.","Using a single holistic 1–5 score; the symptom is reviewers agreeing on the average but rarely on any individual item.","Discussing items as a group before labelling; the symptom is suspiciously high agreement that drops sharply when the same reviewers label a new batch alone.","Reporting raw agreement only; the symptom is \"90% agreement\" on a set where 90% of items are passes and reviewers almost never agree on the failures.","Editing the guideline without versioning labels; the symptom is a metric jump between dataset versions that nobody can attribute to the model or the definition change.","Letting reviewers see which model produced each output; the symptom is the new version winning by more in SME review than in blind or automated checks."],"when":"Write guidelines before any SME labels more than a calibration batch, and before you build an LLM judge,\nsince the judge's prompt should be derived from the same document. Use the full protocol (blind,\nrandomised, double-labelled subset, κ) for acceptance sets and judge calibration; a lighter version is\nfine for exploratory error analysis. Track agreement per criterion, unsure rate and guideline version.\nIn interviews, the strong signal is treating low agreement as a guideline defect and showing how\ndisagreement is attributed to a specific criterion.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the review tool with blinding, per-reviewer shuffling, rationale fields and guideline version capture, and computes per-criterion agreement automatically.","architect":"Makes the guideline the single source for human labels, judge prompts and acceptance criteria, so all three evolve together under version control.","fde":"Drafts the guideline with the customer's SMEs from real cases, runs calibration rounds, and takes recurring splits to the policy owner for a decision."},"check":{"q":"Two SMEs show 88% raw agreement on a pass/fail review. Your manager says the labels are reliable.\nWhat would you check before agreeing?","a":"I would check the label balance and compute a chance-corrected statistic. If, say, 85% of items are\npasses for both reviewers, chance agreement alone is high and κ may be low, meaning they rarely agree on\nwhich items fail, which are exactly the items the eval is about. I would look at agreement restricted to\nitems either reviewer failed, and group disagreements by the guideline criterion cited. If one criterion\ndominates, the guideline definition is ambiguous. I would also confirm reviewers labelled independently\nand blind to system identity, since discussion or version knowledge inflates agreement."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are binary per-criterion labels usually preferred over one 1–5 quality score for SME review?","options":["Binary labels are faster to store and query","Narrow questions get more consistent answers and show which criterion failed","Five-point scales cannot be used with chance-corrected agreement","LLM judges can only output binary labels"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A holistic score lets each reviewer weight criteria privately, so the same output gets different scores. Per-criterion binary labels are easier to define operationally and pinpoint the failure."},{"q":"Raw agreement is 80% but Cohen's kappa is about 0.5. What does that indicate?","options":["The kappa calculation is wrong because agreement is high","The reviewers are careless and should be replaced","Much of the agreement is expected by chance because one label dominates","The dataset is too small for kappa to be meaningful"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Kappa subtracts the agreement expected from each reviewer's label rates. When most items pass, two reviewers agree often by chance alone; the residual agreement on the hard items is what kappa shows."},{"q":"Why should SMEs commit labels independently before discussing disagreements?","options":["Discussion takes too long in a labelling session","Independent labels are required for blinding","Discussion is never useful for annotation","Otherwise the most senior or vocal reviewer's view becomes the label and real ambiguity is hidden"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Independent labels reveal where the guideline is genuinely unclear. Discussion afterwards is valuable for fixing the guideline, but before labelling it manufactures agreement."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/labeling-sessions","sources":[],"why":"SMEs are the bottleneck of every customer evaluation. They are senior, booked, and labelling is not their\njob. The default approach, sharing a spreadsheet or a labelling-tool link with a deadline, produces a\npredictable curve: a burst of labels on day one, then a long tail, with the hardest items skipped\nbecause nobody is there to answer \"what do you mean by this category?\" Weeks pass, and the eval set the\npilot depends on is half done and labelled by whoever had time.\n\nAsynchronous labelling also loses the most valuable output of expert labelling, which is not the labels\nthemselves but the rules they reveal. When two adjusters disagree on a claim and explain why in the same\nroom, you learn the unwritten exception that decides it, and you can write it down while the policy\nowner is present. In async mode that disagreement shows up weeks later as two numbers in a column.\n\nFinally, sessions create accountability that a link cannot: a recurring calendar slot with a sponsor's\nbacking is a commitment the customer's managers can plan around. The naive alternative, a single long\nworkshop, fails too, because fatigue degrades label quality after an hour or two, and a single session\nleaves no room to fix the guideline between rounds.","how":"### Before the session\n1. **Prepare the batch.** Select items from the sampled pool and size it from capacity, not ambition.\n   Label capacity is reviewers × labelling minutes ÷ minutes per label; every reviewer also labels the\n   gold checks, and a double-label fraction d costs d extra labels per item, so\n   `items ≈ (capacity − reviewers × gold_checks) / (1 + d)`. Measure minutes per label in the first\n   session rather than guessing, and shuffle strata through the batch so fatigue late in the session does\n   not fall on one segment. Include a few previously adjudicated \"gold check\" items to detect drift in\n   individual reviewers.\n2. **Pre-load the tool.** Whatever the tool (Label Studio, Argilla, the customer's own platform or a\n   controlled spreadsheet), it must open on the item, show the guideline beside it and capture label,\n   rationale and reviewer. Fiddling with access during the session wastes the scarcest time.\n3. **Clear access.** Confirm every SME can open the data in the environment governance allows, the day\n   before, not at minute one.\n4. **Share the current guideline** and a one-page decision card.\n\n### During the session\n1. **Warm-up calibration (10–15 minutes).** Everyone labels the same handful of items silently, then\n   reveals. This re-anchors reviewers to the guideline *because* standards drift between sessions.\n2. **Independent labelling (the bulk).** Reviewers work alone on assigned items, with a fraction\n   double-assigned for agreement. No discussion yet, *because* talking first turns the loudest opinion\n   into the label.\n3. **Disagreement review.** The facilitator shows double-labelled items where reviewers split, asks each\n   to explain, and drives to one of three outcomes: a guideline clarification, an escalation to the\n   policy owner, or \"genuinely ambiguous\" (flagged and excluded from scoring).\n4. **Decision log.** Each outcome is written into the guideline changelog on the spot, with the item IDs\n   it affects, so relabelling can be targeted later.\n\n### After the session\nAdjudicate remaining splits, relabel items affected by guideline changes, recompute agreement, and send\nthe sponsor a short note: items labelled, agreement, decisions made, open questions. That note is what\nkeeps the next session on the calendar. Read one session's agreement with care: on 30 doubles, 80%\nagreement carries roughly ±14 points (√(0.8·0.2/30) ≈ 0.073), so a drop from 83% to 77% between\nsessions is noise. Track agreement pooled over a rolling window of sessions, and per reviewer pair,\nbefore concluding that a guideline or a reviewer has drifted. The same logic applies to gold checks:\none miss in three is weak evidence on its own and becomes a signal only when it repeats.\n\n### Facilitation choices\nKeep sessions to about 60–90 minutes, *because* label quality decays with fatigue. Have an engineer\npresent to answer \"what is the system actually seeing?\" questions. Do not show model outputs as\npre-labels in calibration batches; pre-labels anchor reviewers, and if you use them later to save time,\nmeasure how often reviewers change them.","example":"A 90-minute weekly session for a contract-review pilot with three in-house lawyers (all timings and rates\nillustrative).\n\n**Prep:** 120 clause-extraction outputs are loaded, 30 of them assigned to two lawyers each, plus 3 gold\ncheck items from last week's adjudication. Access was tested the previous afternoon; one lawyer's\naccount lacked permission and was fixed then.\n\n**0–15 min, calibration:** all three label 5 clauses silently. On one, a limitation-of-liability clause\nwith a carve-out for confidentiality breaches, two mark \"cap correctly extracted\" and one marks \"fail,\ncarve-out omitted\". The guideline did not say whether carve-outs are part of the cap. The general counsel\n(present for this slot) rules that carve-outs must be extracted, and the facilitator adds rule L4 and an\nexample.\n\n**15–65 min, independent labelling:** last week's session measured a little under a minute per label,\nso capacity is 3 lawyers × 50 minutes ≈ 159 labels. Taking off 3 gold checks per lawyer leaves 150,\nand with a 25% double-label fraction that is 150 / 1.25 = 120 items: 90 labelled once and 30 labelled\ntwice. Each lawyer completes about 53 labels, with no slack, which is why the batch was not padded.\n\n**65–85 min, disagreement review:** 6 of the 30 double-labelled items split. Four trace to the meaning of\n\"auto-renewal\" when notice periods differ; this becomes an escalation for the general counsel, due before\nnext week. Two are slips by the same lawyer, who also missed one of the three gold-check items; any one of those\nsignals alone would be weak, but together they justify a second look at that lawyer's batch.\n\n**85–90 min, wrap:** the facilitator reads back two decisions and one open question. The follow-up note\nreports 120 items labelled, 24 of 30 doubles in agreement (80%, a wide interval at this size, so it\nis reported next to the four-week pooled figure), rule L4 added, 9 earlier items flagged for relabel\nunder L4.","tradeoffs":["Live sessions get decisions and throughput, but demand synchronous calendars; when SMEs are across time zones, run async labelling with a short live disagreement review instead.","Having the policy owner attend resolves splits immediately, but their presence can make reviewers defer to them; have them join only for the disagreement segment.","Pre-labelling with model outputs can raise throughput substantially, but anchors reviewers toward the model; use it only after calibration, and track the change rate to detect rubber-stamping.","Longer sessions label more per calendar booking, but quality drops with fatigue; when gold-check accuracy falls in the second half, shorten the session."],"pitfalls":["Spending the first twenty minutes on access and tool problems; the symptom is a session that labels a third of its planned batch and a sponsor questioning the time.","Discussing items before reviewers commit labels; the symptom is near-perfect agreement in session that is not reproduced when the same reviewers label alone.","Making decisions without writing them down; the symptom is the same disagreement being debated again two sessions later.","Not sending a summary to the sponsor; the symptom is SMEs being pulled into other work and sessions quietly cancelled.","Ignoring gold-check items; the symptom is one reviewer's labels drifting for weeks unnoticed and dragging down agreement."],"when":"Run labeling sessions whenever SME labels are on the critical path of a pilot: building the golden set,\ncalibrating an LLM judge, or reviewing a new failure category. A weekly recurring slot is usually better\nthan an occasional workshop because it allows guideline fixes between rounds. Pure async labelling is\nfine for stable, well-calibrated tasks with high agreement. Measure items per session, agreement on\ndoubles, gold-check accuracy, unsure rate and decisions logged. In interviews, describe how you keep\nlabels independent while still extracting rules from disagreement.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Prepares batches, gold checks and double assignments, makes sure the tool captures rationale and guideline version, and computes agreement immediately after the session.","architect":"Chooses labelling tooling and where it runs under the customer's data constraints, and designs how session decisions flow into guidelines, judge prompts and datasets.","fde":"Books and facilitates sessions, keeps the sponsor informed, steers disagreements to decisions, and protects SME time from tooling and access problems."},"check":{"q":"Your sessions label plenty of items but agreement on double-labelled items keeps falling week over\nweek. What might be wrong and what would you change?","a":"First I would confirm the fall is real: with about 30 doubles per session, single-session\nagreement swings by over ten points on noise alone, so I would compare pooled multi-session windows. If\nit holds, falling agreement with steady throughput suggests drift rather than a bad guideline: reviewers'\nprivate standards diverging, new item types in the batches that the guideline does not cover, or fatigue.\nI would check gold-check accuracy per reviewer to find an individual drifting, look at which guideline\nsections are cited in recent disagreements to find uncovered cases, and compare agreement in the first\nversus second half of sessions for fatigue. Fixes would be a longer warm-up calibration, guideline\nsections plus examples for the new case types, and shorter sessions if quality decays late."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why start each labeling session with a short silent calibration on shared items?","options":["To measure how fast each reviewer works","To re-anchor reviewers to the guideline and surface new ambiguities before the bulk of labelling","To generate pre-labels for the rest of the batch","To test whether the labelling tool is working"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Standards drift between sessions. A few shared items labelled silently, then revealed, show where reviewers diverge so the guideline can be clarified before hundreds of labels inherit the split."},{"q":"What is the main risk of showing model outputs as pre-labels to SMEs?","options":["It slows the session down","It exposes customer data to the model","It makes agreement statistics impossible to compute","Reviewers anchor on the pre-label, so labels drift toward the model's view"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Anchoring makes reviewers accept plausible model outputs they would have failed unaided. If pre-labels are used for throughput, measure how often reviewers change them."},{"q":"During disagreement review, two SMEs split for a reason the guideline does not cover. What is the best outcome?","options":["Take the more senior SME's label","Average the labels and move on to save time","Remove the item permanently from the dataset","Record a guideline decision or escalation with affected item IDs so labels can be updated"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The value of the session is turning splits into written rules. Logging the decision with item IDs lets the team relabel precisely and stops the same debate recurring."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/eval-review-loops","sources":[],"why":"After the golden set and a first prompt exist, the tempting workflow is \"change the prompt, re-run, watch\nthe number\". That treats the eval as an oracle. It is not. In a customer deployment, a failing case can\nmean three different things: the system is wrong; the reference label is wrong; or the guideline never\ndefined the case, so neither is clearly right. Early in a project the second and third are common,\nbecause the dataset was labelled before anyone saw how the system fails. If you only tune the system,\nyou will \"fix\" correct behaviour to match wrong labels, and the customer's experts will reject the\nresult in production.\n\nThere is a second reason. Most customer evals at scale use an automated grader, often an LLM judge,\nbecause SMEs cannot review every output of every run. The judge drifts from the experts in ways that\nare invisible from the score: lenient on confident wrong answers, harsh on terse correct ones. Only\nperiodically comparing judge verdicts against SME verdicts on the same items keeps the automated number\nhonest. And third, a score without review builds no shared understanding with the customer. The loop is\nwhere the customer's experts see failures, agree on what matters, and come to trust the number used at\nacceptance.","how":"### One iteration\n1. **Run** the current system version on the iteration set (never the holdout) and grade with the\n   automated grader. Record system version, prompt version, dataset version and grader version together,\n   *because* a score is only comparable when all four are pinned.\n2. **Select for review.** All failures in high-stakes strata, a random sample of other failures, and a\n   random sample of *passes*. Passes are included *because* grader leniency only shows up there.\n3. **Read and code failures.** Engineers and SMEs read each selected item and assign a cause from a\n   small, evolving taxonomy (retrieval missed the clause, misread table, wrong policy precedence, format\n   violation, and so on). Open coding first, then cluster; a fixed taxonomy invented upfront misses the\n   failure modes you did not anticipate.\n4. **Attribute each item.** System error, label error, guideline gap, or grader error. This is the step\n   that distinguishes a review loop from error analysis alone.\n5. **Route the fix.** System errors go to the prompt, retrieval or code backlog, ordered by frequency\n   times cost. Label errors go to relabelling with SME sign-off. Guideline gaps go to the policy owner,\n   then the guideline, labels and judge prompt are updated together. Grader errors go to judge\n   recalibration.\n6. **Re-run with regression checks.** Compare per-stratum results against the previous version, and\n   inspect every item that flipped from pass to fail, *because* averages hide regressions offset by\n   gains elsewhere.\n7. **Report.** A short note to the customer: score with interval, top failure clusters, decisions taken,\n   what changed in labels or guideline (so score movement is explainable).\n\n### How much to read, and in what order\nRead failures until new cause categories stop appearing; in practice the first few dozen failures\nusually reveal the main clusters, and reading hundreds more mostly adds counts, not categories. Then\nrank clusters by frequency × cost of the error in this customer's process, *because* a rare error that\nsends money to the wrong bank account outranks a common formatting slip.\n\n### Reading the score through the review\nThe review sample also corrects the headline. If the judge passes a share p_judge of items, SMEs find\nthat a fraction f_pass of judge-passes are actually wrong, and a fraction r_fail of judge-fails are\nactually right (label or grader errors), then the SME-equivalent pass rate is approximately\n\n```\np_true ≈ p_judge × (1 − f_pass) + (1 − p_judge) × r_fail\n```\n\nBoth fractions come from small samples, so the corrected number carries a wide band; its value is in\nshowing which direction the grader errs and by how much.\n\n### Keeping the grader honest\nEach cycle, have SMEs grade a fixed-size sample that the judge also graded, and track judge-to-SME\nagreement over time. A drop means the judge prompt or the guideline it encodes has fallen behind. The\nsample must be random rather than drawn from failures, or it measures the judge only where it is\nalready suspected.\n\n### Avoiding overfitting\nThe loop repeatedly exposes the iteration set. Keep a holdout that nobody reads, and evaluate on it only\nat milestones. A large gap between iteration and holdout scores means the team has tuned to specific\ncases rather than the behaviour.\n\n### Cadence\nWeekly is a common rhythm during a pilot: frequent enough to keep momentum, slow enough for SMEs. Rerun\nthe automated eval on every change; reserve the human review for the cadence.","example":"A three-week loop on a support-answer assistant for a software customer; numbers illustrative. The\niteration set has 300 items, the holdout 100. An LLM judge grades correctness against references.\n\n**Week 1.** Judge pass rate 78%. The team reviews all 66 failures plus 30 random passes with two SMEs.\nOf the 66 failures: 31 are system errors (19 retrieval returned an outdated release note, 12 wrong\nproduct-tier logic), 14 are label errors (references written before a pricing change), 9 are guideline\ngaps (is a workaround an acceptable answer when a fix exists?) and 12 are judge errors (correct answers\nmarked wrong because they were shorter than the reference). Among the 30 passes, 4 are actually wrong,\njudged lenient because they sounded confident.\n\nReading the score through the review: f_pass ≈ 4/30 ≈ 0.13, and r_fail = 26/66 ≈ 0.39 (the label and\njudge errors, where the system was right). So p_true ≈ 0.78 × 0.87 + 0.22 × 0.39 ≈ 0.76, before the 9\nguideline gaps are ruled on. The headline was roughly right by accident: about 30 false passes and 26\nfalse fails nearly cancel. A grader whose errors cancel on this mix will not cancel on the next one, so\nthe judge is fixed anyway.\n\nDecisions: filter retrieval by release version; relabel 14 items; product owner rules \"a workaround is\nacceptable only if the answer also states the fix version\"; judge prompt updated to grade content not\nlength and to check stated version numbers.\n\n**Week 2.** After relabelling and the judge update, the *same* week-1 system scores 81%; the\nimprovement is measurement, not the model, and is reported as such. It is smaller than the 26 recovered\nlabel and length errors alone would suggest (that would be about 87%) because the stricter version check\nalso turns some previously lenient passes into failures; the week-1 pass sample implied there were\ndozens of them. With the retrieval fix, 87%. Two\nitems flip pass-to-fail; both involve multi-version questions now filtered too aggressively, so the\nfilter is relaxed for those queries.\n\n**Week 3.** 89% on the iteration set and 86% on the holdout, a small gap and well inside the roughly\n±7-point 95% interval a 100-item holdout carries. Judge-to-SME agreement on a fresh random 40-item\nsample is 36 of 40, up from 31 of 40 on the equivalent random sample in week 1. Forty items is a small\nsample, so the team treats this as consistent with the fixes rather than proof, and keeps sampling\nweekly. The customer now trusts the number enough to use it in the acceptance test.","tradeoffs":["Deep SME review of every failure gives accurate attribution, but consumes expert time; once judge and SME agree well, review a sample of failures and all high-stakes ones.","Fixing labels and guidelines makes the score more truthful, but moves the baseline, so trends are harder to read; always re-score the previous system version on the new labels before comparing.","A weekly cadence keeps SMEs engaged, but can feel slow to engineers; run automated evals continuously and keep human review weekly.","An evolving failure taxonomy captures new failure modes, but makes cluster counts incomparable over time; freeze it at a milestone once new categories stop appearing."],"pitfalls":["Treating every failure as a system error; the symptom is the team rewriting prompts to match a reference that SMEs later admit was wrong.","Reviewing only failures; the symptom is a judge that is lenient on confident wrong answers and a production complaint rate higher than the eval suggests.","Comparing scores across label or judge changes without re-scoring the old system; the symptom is a celebrated improvement that came from relabelling, not engineering.","Repeatedly tuning on the same visible items; the symptom is a widening gap between iteration and holdout scores.","Silently editing labels; the symptom is the customer asking why last week's 82% is now 79% with no system change and nobody being able to explain it."],"when":"Run an eval review loop from the first time you have a system, a golden set and an automated grader,\nand keep it running through the pilot and into production with real traffic samples. It is the main\nmechanism for converting SME expertise into system improvements and a trusted acceptance metric. Skip\nthe heavy human part only when grader-to-SME agreement is high and stable and the task is not\nhigh-stakes. Measure the attribution mix over time; label and guideline errors should shrink as the\ndataset matures. In interviews, the distinguishing answer separates system, label, guideline and grader\nerrors.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Pins system, prompt, dataset and grader versions per run, builds the flip report and review queue, and turns failure clusters into prioritised fixes.","architect":"Designs the eval platform so versions, holdouts and judge-to-SME agreement are tracked per customer, making scores comparable and auditable across releases.","fde":"Runs the weekly review with the customer's SMEs, routes guideline gaps to the policy owner, and explains every score movement in the customer report."},"check":{"q":"Your judge-scored pass rate jumped from 74% to 85% in one week. The engineers changed the prompt;\nthe SMEs also relabelled 30 items and the judge prompt was edited. How much of the gain is the prompt?","a":"I cannot tell from the two numbers because three things changed at once. I would re-run the\nprevious system version with the new labels and the new judge; the difference between that and 74% is\nthe measurement change. The difference between that re-score and 85% is the prompt effect. I would also\ncheck flips in both directions, verify the judge edit with an SME agreement sample, and confirm the\nholdout moved in the same direction. Only the prompt-attributable part should be reported as system\nimprovement to the customer."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should an eval review include a random sample of passing items?","options":["Passing items are where the system has the most errors","Grader leniency only shows up in items it marked as passes","SMEs need easy items to stay motivated","Including passes raises the measured pass rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the judge accepts confident wrong answers, those errors never appear in the failure list. Reviewing passes estimates false passes and reveals where the grader needs recalibration."},{"q":"A failure is traced to a reference answer written before a policy change. What is the right action?","options":["Change the prompt so the system reproduces the old reference","Delete the item from the dataset","Relabel it with SME sign-off, version the dataset, and re-score the previous system","Leave it, since the score only needs to be directionally right"],"answer":2,"explanation":"This is a label error, not a system error. Fixing it with sign-off and re-scoring the old version keeps comparisons honest and stops the team tuning the system toward outdated behaviour."},{"q":"What does a growing gap between the iteration-set score and the holdout score indicate?","options":["The holdout set is mislabelled","The automated judge is becoming more accurate","Production traffic has drifted","The team is overfitting fixes to the specific cases it repeatedly reviews"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The iteration set is read every cycle, so fixes can become case-specific. The untouched holdout estimates generalisation; a widening gap means improvements are not transferring."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/acceptance-testing-against-customer-kpis","sources":[],"why":"A customer buys an outcome, not an accuracy number. The sponsor's success statement is in business terms:\ncut average handle time, raise first-contact resolution, process more claims per adjuster, keep error\nrates no worse than today. Engineering teams naturally report what they can measure, model-level\nmetrics, and the two languages do not meet. A 91% extraction accuracy might be great or useless depending\non which fields fail, how errors are caught downstream and what the human process achieved before.\n\nThree failures follow when acceptance is left informal. First, there is no baseline: nobody measured the\ncurrent process, so \"better\" has no reference and the human error rate is assumed to be zero. Second,\nthe bar moves: once results are in, stakeholders who are unhappy for other reasons re-open the\ndefinition of success. Third, the statistics are ignored: a pilot of 200 cases \"beats\" a 2% error target\nwith 1.5%, yet the uncertainty on 200 cases is wider than the difference. The naive alternatives,\naccepting on a demo or on a single overall accuracy number, either prove nothing or prove the wrong\nthing. Acceptance testing exists to make the deal explicit: these metrics, measured this way, on this\ndata, with these thresholds and this much confidence, mean the system is accepted.","how":"### 1. Start from the KPI tree\nWrite the sponsor's KPI at the top (cost per invoice processed), decompose it into drivers (share of\ninvoices needing no human touch, review time per touched invoice, error rate reaching payment), and map\neach driver to something measurable offline or in a pilot. Offline evals can measure accuracy and\nstraight-through eligibility; only a pilot can measure time and behaviour change. Doing the tree\nexplicitly matters *because* it shows which model metric moves which business number.\n\n### 2. Measure the baseline\nMeasure the current process on the same kind of sample: human error rate, handle time, throughput.\nWithout it, thresholds are guesses and the system is held to a standard of perfection the humans never\nmet.\n\n### 3. Define criteria of three types\n- **Primary:** the KPI proxies that must improve (straight-through rate, handle time).\n- **Guardrails:** metrics that must not get worse (critical-field error rate, compliance violations,\n  escalation rate), *because* optimising one KPI often degrades another.\n- **Segment floors:** minimum performance on high-stakes strata (high-value invoices, regulated\n  products), *because* an overall average can pass while the segment that matters fails.\n\n### 4. Fix the statistics in advance\nFor each criterion, state whether the threshold applies to the point estimate or to a confidence bound,\nand size the sample accordingly. For proportions near 0 or 1, use an interval that behaves well there,\nsuch as the Wilson interval; the simple normal approximation can produce bounds below zero. Sample\nsize must be agreed with this in mind *because* some criteria are unprovable at small n.\n\nThree decisions hide inside \"no worse than today\":\n- **Non-inferiority margin.** Requiring the system's upper bound to sit at or below the baseline's\n  point estimate demands near-proof that it is *better*. The usual form is non-inferiority: upper bound\n  ≤ baseline + δ, where δ is the largest degradation the sponsor would accept. Choosing δ is a business\n  decision, not a statistical one, so it is the sponsor's to sign.\n- **Baseline uncertainty.** The baseline is itself a sample. If it was measured on a few hundred cases,\n  its own interval may be wider than the margin; either measure it on more cases or acknowledge that the\n  comparison is approximate.\n- **Power.** To show an upper bound at or below threshold t when the true rate is p, with z_α for the\n  bound and z_β for the chance of passing, roughly\n  n ≈ [z_α·√(t(1−t)) + z_β·√(p(1−p))]² / (t − p)². The (t − p)² in the denominator is the lesson: halving\n  the gap between expected performance and threshold quadruples the sample.\n\nFinally, if acceptance requires *every* criterion to pass, the chances multiply: three independent\ncriteria each passed with 90% probability by a system that truly meets them are all passed only about\n73% of the time (0.9³). That is a reason to keep the signed list short and to power each criterion\ngenerously.\n\n### 5. Sign before measuring\nPut metrics, data, thresholds, sample sizes and the decision rule in the statement of work or an\nacceptance annex, signed by the sponsor. Freeze the acceptance dataset version and the holdout.\n\n### 6. Run in stages\nOffline on the frozen golden holdout, then shadow mode or a limited pilot on live traffic with humans\nstill in control, then a pilot that measures time and throughput against the baseline or a concurrent\ncontrol group. Each stage has its own pass criteria, and failure sends you back to the review loop.\n\n### 7. Report against the contract\nReport every criterion with its estimate, interval and pass or fail, plus segment results. Do not\nsubstitute a different metric that happens to look better.","example":"Invoice extraction for an accounts-payable team. Baseline, measured on 500 invoices: 2.0% critical-field\nerror rate (amount, supplier bank details, due date), and every invoice is touched by a human. The KPI is\ncost per invoice; the agreed proxies are below, all illustrative.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef wilson(k, n, z=1.96):\n    \"\"\"95% Wilson interval for a proportion k/n (better than the normal approximation near 0 or 1).\"\"\"\n    p, d = k / n, 1 + z * z / n\n    centre = (p + z * z / (2 * n)) / d\n    half = z * math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n + z * z / (4 * n * n)) / d\n    return centre - half, centre + half\n\n# Criteria signed in the SOW: (name, observed count, n, rule, threshold)\nCRITERIA = [\n    (\"critical-field error rate\", 6, 400, \"upper<=\", 0.02),   # guardrail: no worse than baseline\n    (\"straight-through rate\", 292, 400, \"lower>=\", 0.65),     # primary: no human touch needed\n    (\"critical-field error, high-value\", 3, 80, \"upper<=\", 0.03),  # segment floor\n]\nfor name, k, n, rule, t in CRITERIA:\n    lo, hi = wilson(k, n)\n    ok = hi <= t if rule == \"upper<=\" else lo >= t\n    print(f\"{name:34s} {k/n:6.1%} CI [{lo:.1%}, {hi:.1%}] {rule}{t:.0%} {'PASS' if ok else 'FAIL'}\")\n# critical-field error rate            1.5% CI [0.7%, 3.2%] upper<=2% FAIL\n# straight-through rate               73.0% CI [68.4%, 77.1%] lower>=65% PASS\n# critical-field error, high-value     3.8% CI [1.3%, 10.5%] upper<=3% FAIL\n```\n\nThe point estimate of 1.5% beats the 2% baseline, but 400 invoices cannot show it with confidence; with\na true rate of 1.5%, about 3,000 invoices are needed just for the upper bound at the expected error\ncount to reach 2%, and at that size the test still passes only about half the time; roughly 8,000\ninvoices are needed for around a 90% chance of passing. The criterion was also stricter than its\nlabel: the baseline was 10 errors in 500, whose own Wilson interval is roughly 1.1% to 3.6%, so \"upper\nbound ≤ 2.0%\" asked for proof of improvement over a noisy number. A non-inferiority version, upper bound\n≤ 2.5% (a 0.5-point margin the AP manager signs as tolerable), needs only about 2,000 to 2,500 invoices\nfor a roughly 90% chance of passing at a true 1.5%, a volume pilot traffic can often supply. The\nsegment floor is worse: even zero errors in 80 invoices gives an upper bound near 4.6%, so that\ncriterion could never pass at that size. All of this should have surfaced when the annex was written.\nThe fix is a renegotiation *before* testing: either larger samples (pilot traffic can supply them), or a\npoint-estimate rule backed by a downstream control such as human review of all high-value invoices.","tradeoffs":["Confidence-bound thresholds protect the customer from lucky small samples, but demand large test sets; when labelled data is scarce, use point estimates plus a production guardrail that triggers rollback.","Concurrent control groups give a clean causal estimate of time saved, but cost the customer capacity running two processes; a before-and-after comparison is cheaper when volume and mix are stable.","A wider non-inferiority margin makes the test passable at realistic sample sizes, but admits a system that is somewhat worse than today; when errors are costly or regulated, keep δ small and pay for the larger sample instead.","Many criteria cover more risks, but each extra criterion raises the chance of failing on noise; keep a handful of primary metrics and guardrails, and report the rest as diagnostics.","Strict segment floors protect high-stakes cases, but can block launch on tiny segments; when a segment is too small to test, route it to humans and exclude it from automated scope."],"pitfalls":["Setting thresholds without a measured baseline; the symptom is a system rejected for a 2% error rate when the human process, once measured, turns out to run higher.","Agreeing thresholds after seeing results; the symptom is a sponsor or a skeptical stakeholder re-opening the definition of success at the go/no-go meeting.","Only primary metrics, no guardrails; the symptom is a faster process that pushes more errors into payment or compliance, discovered at month-end.","Criteria that are unprovable at the agreed sample size; the symptom is a \"fail\" on a system whose observed performance beats the threshold.","Accepting on offline evals alone; the symptom is good golden-set numbers and no change in handle time, because users do not trust the output and re-check everything."],"when":"Define acceptance tests at scoping, as soon as the KPIs and baseline are known, and certainly before the\npilot starts; they drive dataset size, sampling strata and pilot design. Use offline criteria for\nquality and pilot criteria for time, throughput and behaviour. Skip formal statistics only for\nlow-stakes internal tools where the cost of a wrong decision is a cheap rollback. Track each criterion\nwith its interval over the pilot, not just at the end. In interviews, show the chain from business KPI\nto proxy to threshold to sample size, and mention baselines and guardrails unprompted.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the acceptance harness that computes every signed criterion with intervals and segment breakdowns from frozen dataset versions, reproducibly.","architect":"Owns the KPI tree and decides which criteria are tested offline, in shadow mode, or in a live pilot, and what production guardrails back them after launch.","fde":"Negotiates the acceptance annex with the sponsor, insists on a baseline, and checks that thresholds are provable at the agreed sample size before anything is signed."},"check":{"q":"A sponsor wants the contract to say \"the AI must be 99% accurate\". How do you turn that into an\nacceptance test you can actually pass or fail fairly?","a":"I would ask what business outcome 99% stands for, then decompose it: which errors matter (a wrong\nbank account is not a misspelled supplier name), what the current human error rate is, and which KPI\nthe sponsor is buying, such as time or cost per item. I would propose a primary metric tied to that KPI,\nguardrails on critical-field errors framed as non-inferiority against the measured baseline, with a\nmargin the sponsor signs, and floors on high-stakes segments. For each I would state whether the threshold applies to a point estimate or a confidence bound\nand size the sample so it is provable. Then everything goes into a signed annex with a frozen dataset."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why measure the human baseline before setting acceptance thresholds?","options":["Baselines are required for statistical significance tests","The AI system must always beat humans by a fixed margin","Without it, thresholds are guesses and the system may be held to a standard the current process never met","Baselines replace the need for a golden dataset"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Human processes have error rates and costs too. Measuring them grounds thresholds in reality and turns \"is it perfect?\" into \"is it better than what we do today?\""},{"q":"An acceptance criterion says the error rate's 95% upper bound must be at most 3%, tested on 80 items. What is the problem?","options":["80 items is too many for a segment test","Upper bounds should never be used for error rates","The Wilson interval cannot be computed at small n","Even zero errors on 80 items leaves an upper bound above 3%, so the criterion cannot pass"],"answer":3,"explanation":"With n = 80, the Wilson upper bound for zero errors is about 4.6%. The test is unpassable by design, which should be caught when the criteria and sample sizes are agreed."},{"q":"What is the role of guardrail metrics in a KPI acceptance test?","options":["They replace primary metrics when those are hard to measure","They ensure improving the target KPI does not degrade something else that matters, such as errors","They are only reported after launch","They measure the model's latency and nothing else"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pushing straight-through rate or speed can increase downstream errors or compliance breaches. Guardrails make \"no worse than baseline\" on those dimensions an explicit condition of acceptance."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/customer-specific-regression-suites","sources":[],"why":"Once one product serves many customers, every shared component (base prompt, model version, retriever,\nparser) is a change to all of them at once. The team's global eval set is usually a blend of cases from\nmany sources, and a blend answers \"did we get better on average?\" Customers never experience the\naverage. They experience their own traffic: their invoice layout, their label taxonomy, their rule that\namounts are always written with two decimals.\n\nThe naive approach is one big golden set with some cases from each customer. It fails through dilution.\nIf customer A contributes 120 of 3,000 cases (4%) and a change drops A from 92% to 80% pass, the\nglobal score moves by about half a point, which is inside the run-to-run noise of most LLM evals. The\nrelease ships, and the first signal is an angry email from A's operations lead. The second naive\napproach, \"re-run the customer's acceptance test before each release\", fails differently: acceptance\ntests were written once, for go-live, and do not contain the bugs found since. The bugs a customer has\nalready reported are the ones they will notice first if they come back, so they are the most valuable\ntests you own.\n\nA per-customer suite fixes both: results are reported and gated per customer, so a regression for one\ncustomer cannot be averaged away by gains for others, and the suite grows with every fixed incident.","how":"### What goes into a suite\nA suite is assembled from four sources, each for a different reason:\n1. **Acceptance cases.** The cases that defined \"done\" at go-live (see acceptance testing against KPIs).\n   They protect the contractual baseline.\n2. **Fixed incidents.** Every production failure that was diagnosed and fixed becomes a case, *after*\n   the fix, with the assertion that would have caught it. This is the \"every bug becomes a test\" rule,\n   and it is why the suite gets more valuable over time.\n3. **Contract rules.** Format, terminology, refusal and escalation behaviours the customer requires\n   (\"never state a settlement amount\", \"dates as DD/MM/YYYY\"). These are often deterministic checks.\n4. **Sampled traffic.** A small stratified sample of recent real inputs, so the suite does not drift\n   away from what the customer actually sends.\n\n### What a case records\nEach case stores the input, the **customer configuration it must run under** (tenant flags, prompt\nversion, retrieval index snapshot or fixed context, tool stubs), the assertion type (exact match,\nschema check, field-level comparison, rubric or LLM judge), a **severity tier** (blocking or tracked),\nprovenance (which ticket or SME session it came from), and an owner. Configuration is part of the case\nbecause a regression that only appears with A's feature flags is invisible if you test with defaults.\nRetrieval is frozen (a snapshot or the captured context) because otherwise a failure could be caused by\nnew documents rather than by your change, and you could not tell which.\n\n### How it is run and judged\nOn every change to a shared component, the harness runs each customer's suite under that customer's\nconfig, for both the **candidate** and the **current production version**. Comparison is paired, case by\ncase, because the same cases are run on both sides: what matters is which cases flipped from pass to\nfail and which flipped the other way. Paired comparison is far more sensitive than comparing two pass\nrates, because case difficulty cancels out. The gate has two parts:\n- **Blocking cases must pass.** A contract-rule or previously-fixed-incident case that fails blocks the\n  release for that customer, regardless of averages.\n- **Scored cases must not regress beyond noise.** Count pass-to-fail and fail-to-pass flips and test\n  whether the imbalance is larger than chance (McNemar's test on the discordant pairs). Measure noise by\n  running production against itself a few times; cases that flip between identical runs are flaky and\n  should be fixed or repeated, not gated on.\n\n### Power and many customers\nTwo statistical facts shape the gate. First, McNemar only sees discordant pairs, so a small suite has\nlittle power: with 3 flips against and 0 for, the exact two-sided p is 0.25, so a pure significance gate\nwaves through a change that broke three of a customer's cases and fixed none. That is why the blocking\ntier exists and why many teams add a practical floor (\"no more than N net pass-to-fail flips\") alongside\nthe test. Second, a 40-customer fleet tested at p < 0.05 each will, on a harmless change, falsely block\nat least one customer on most releases (if the tests were independent, 1 − 0.95⁴⁰ ≈ 0.87). Either\ncorrect for it (a Holm or false-discovery-rate adjustment across customers) or treat a significant-but-\nsmall flip count as \"inspect\", not \"block\", and reserve hard blocks for blocking cases.\n\n### Cost and cadence\nCost is customers × cases × repetitions, so suites are tiered: a small smoke subset per customer on every\npull request, the full suite nightly and before release. A failing customer can be held on the old\nversion through per-tenant routing while the rest roll forward, which is only possible if config and\nversion are per tenant.\n\n### Data handling\nSuites contain customer data. They are stored and run under that customer's data agreement and region,\nnever shared with other tenants' suites or used for training without permission, and deleted or\nreturned when the contract ends.","example":"A shared extraction prompt change lifts the global score from 91.2% to 92.0%. Customer A's 120-case\nsuite tells a different story. The paired comparison below counts flips and computes an exact McNemar\np-value from the discordant pairs.\n\n```python\nfrom math import comb\n\ndef mcnemar_exact(b, c):\n    \"\"\"b = pass->fail flips, c = fail->pass flips. Two-sided exact binomial test.\"\"\"\n    n, k = b + c, min(b, c)\n    tail = sum(comb(n, i) for i in range(k + 1)) / 2 ** n\n    return min(1.0, 2 * tail)\n\ndef gate(baseline, candidate, blocking_ids):\n    \"\"\"baseline/candidate: {case_id: bool passed}. Returns (ok, report).\"\"\"\n    broken_blockers = [cid for cid in blocking_ids if not candidate[cid]]\n    b = sum(baseline[k] and not candidate[k] for k in baseline)\n    c = sum(not baseline[k] and candidate[k] for k in baseline)\n    p = mcnemar_exact(b, c)\n    ok = not broken_blockers and not (b > c and p < 0.05)\n    return ok, {\"pass_to_fail\": b, \"fail_to_pass\": c, \"p\": round(p, 4),\n                \"broken_blockers\": broken_blockers}\n\n# Customer A: 14 cases broke, 3 improved, one broken case is a past incident (blocking).\nprint(mcnemar_exact(14, 3))   # 2 * (1 + 17 + 136 + 680) / 2**17 = 0.0127\n```\n\nWith 14 pass-to-fail and 3 fail-to-pass, p ≈ 0.013: the drop is real, not noise, even though A's pass\nrate only moved from, say, 90% to 81% and the global number went up. One of the broken cases was a\nblocking past-incident case, so the gate fails for A on two counts. The fix is to hold A on the old\nprompt version through per-tenant routing, inspect the 14 flips (here, multi-currency invoices), and\nre-run before promoting.","tradeoffs":["Per-customer gating catches regressions averages hide, but cost and run time grow with the number of customers; tiered smoke-versus-full runs keep pull requests fast until the customer count is large.","Freezing retrieval context makes failures attributable to your change, but stops the suite from catching regressions caused by index or ingestion changes; run a separate live-index suite for those.","Gating on every customer slows shared releases; per-tenant version pinning lets most customers move forward, at the price of maintaining several live versions for a while.","LLM-judge assertions cover open-ended outputs that exact checks cannot, but add their own noise; prefer deterministic checks whenever the customer's requirement can be stated as a rule."],"pitfalls":["Running every customer's cases with default configuration; the symptom is a green suite followed by a regression that only occurs under one tenant's feature flags or prompt overrides.","Comparing pass rates instead of paired flips; the symptom is releases blocked by noise one week and real regressions waved through the next, because two independent rates are too noisy at 100-case scale.","Never removing or re-reviewing cases; the symptom is a suite full of expectations the customer no longer holds, and engineers learning to ignore failures as \"probably stale\".","Adding the incident case before the fix is verified, or with the wrong expected output; the symptom is a blocking case that fails on every candidate and gets silently downgraded to tracked.","Applying an uncorrected p < 0.05 gate to every customer in a large fleet; the symptom is that nearly every release blocks some customer whose failing flips, on inspection, share no cause and vanish on re-run, until the team stops trusting the gate.","Treating customer suites as shared fixtures; the symptom is one customer's documents appearing in another customer's test logs or in a training set, which is a data-handling breach."],"when":"Build a per-customer suite as soon as a second customer runs on shared components, and start it on day\none with the acceptance cases. Grow it with every fixed incident. It matters most when customers differ\nin document formats, terminology or contract rules, and when shared prompts or models change often. With\none customer, your global regression set already is the customer suite. Measure flips per customer,\nblocking-case failures and flaky-case rate. In interviews, the strong signal is explaining why averages\nhide per-customer regressions and why the comparison must be paired against production.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the harness that runs each suite under its tenant config, freezes retrieval context, computes paired flips and wires the per-customer gate into CI.","architect":"Makes configuration and model version per tenant so a failing customer can be held back, and sets the data boundary for where customer suites live.","fde":"Converts every fixed customer incident into a blocking case with the customer's SME, and uses the suite results to tell the customer exactly what a release changes for them."},"check":{"q":"A shared prompt change improves the global eval by 1.5 points. One customer's 80-case suite shows\n6 cases newly failing and 2 newly passing. Should you ship, and to whom?","a":"Look at the paired flips, not the averages. Six versus two on 8 discordant pairs gives an exact\nMcNemar p of about 0.29, so on its own that imbalance is not clearly beyond noise; I would check the\nproduction-versus-production flake rate for that suite. But the type of case matters more: if any of the\nsix is a blocking contract rule or a past incident, that customer is held on the old version through\nper-tenant routing while everyone else gets the change. I would then read the six failures to see if\nthey share a cause, fix or add a customer-scoped override, and re-run before promoting that tenant."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are customer regression suites compared case by case against production rather than against a pass-rate threshold?","options":["Thresholds are illegal to set in customer contracts","Paired comparison cancels out case difficulty, so flips reveal real regressions that noisy rates hide","Production outputs are always correct, so they serve as the ground truth labels","Pass-rate thresholds cannot be computed for LLM judges"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Running the same cases on both versions turns the question into which cases flipped, which is much more sensitive than comparing two noisy rates. Production is not ground truth; it is the paired baseline."},{"q":"Which source of cases most directly prevents a customer from seeing the same bug twice?","options":["A random sample of the global eval set","Synthetic cases generated from the product documentation","Each fixed production incident added as a case with the assertion that would have caught it","The acceptance tests written at go-live"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Acceptance tests predate the bugs found in production. Turning each fixed incident into a blocking case guards exactly the failures the customer has already noticed and will check first."},{"q":"Why should each case store the customer's configuration and a frozen retrieval context?","options":["To reduce the token cost of running the suite","Because LLM judges require a configuration object to score outputs","So the suite can be reused for other customers with similar documents","So failures reproduce under that tenant's real setup and are attributable to the change being tested"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Bugs often appear only under one tenant's flags or prompt overrides, and live retrieval adds changes unrelated to the release. Pinning both makes a flip mean that your change caused it."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/shadow-mode-validation","sources":[],"why":"Before go-live, or before swapping a model under a live customer, you have a golden set and a\nregression suite. Both are curated: someone chose which cases to include, and that choice is biased\ntoward cases people thought of. Real traffic contains the long tail nobody wrote down: scanned faxes,\nmixed languages, users pasting entire email threads, queries the customer's own SMEs would not have\npredicted. The question a customer asks is \"will it work on *our* traffic?\", and only their traffic\ncan answer it.\n\nThe naive alternatives each fail. Releasing to everyone and watching metrics exposes every user to an\nunvalidated system, which many customers (regulated ones especially) will not accept. A canary or A/B\ntest exposes a fraction of users, which is better, but still lets real users act on candidate output\nbefore you know it is sound. Replaying last month's logs offline gets the distribution right but goes\nstale and misses things only visible live: latency under real load, rate limits, timeouts, new document\ntypes arriving this week.\n\nShadow mode sits between offline evaluation and exposure: live distribution, live infrastructure, zero\nuser impact. It is a common last step before a canary, and for a forward-deployed team it is often\nthe evidence that gets a customer's risk committee to approve the switch.","how":"### 1. Fork the traffic\nEach production request is handled by the current system as usual. A copy, possibly a sampled\nfraction, is sent to the candidate. There are two ways to fork:\n- **Asynchronous fork (preferred).** The production path finishes and returns first; the request and\n  the production output are published to a queue, and a shadow worker runs the candidate later. Because\n  the candidate is off the request path, its latency, errors or crashes cannot affect users.\n- **Inline fork.** The candidate is called concurrently in the same request, with a strict timeout and\n  its result discarded. This measures latency under identical conditions, but a careless implementation\n  (awaiting the shadow call, sharing a connection pool or rate-limit quota) can slow production down.\n\n### 2. Neutralise side effects\nThis is the step that makes shadow mode safe or dangerous. A candidate agent that sends emails, writes\nrecords or calls payment APIs must not do so in shadow. Tool adapters run in **dry-run mode**: read-only\ntools may execute (or be served from the production call's recorded results), and write tools record\nthe intended call and return a stub. The intended calls are themselves valuable data: \"the candidate\nwould have refunded order 4417\" is something you compare against what production did.\n\n### 3. Capture comparable records\nFor each request, log the input, both outputs, both latencies, token cost, errors, and the versions of\neverything (model, prompt, index). Shadow data is still customer data: it goes to the same store, under\nthe same retention and access rules, and sending it to a *new* model or vendor needs the same data\napproval as production would. Mirroring traffic to an unapproved provider \"only for testing\" is still\na data transfer.\n\n### 4. Compare\n- **Deterministic fields** (extracted values, classifications, routing decisions, tool calls): compute\n  exact agreement rate and a confusion matrix between production and candidate.\n- **Free-text outputs:** agreement is meaningless at the string level, so sample the disagreements and\n  have an LLM judge or SME do a pairwise comparison: candidate better, production better, equivalent.\n- **Operational metrics:** p50/p95 latency, error and timeout rate, cost per request.\n\nFocus human review on disagreements, because that is where the information is; where both systems agree,\nboth are usually right (or both wrong in ways the regression suite should cover).\n\nCalibrate agreement against a **self-agreement baseline**: shadow production against itself (same model,\nprompt and inputs, a second call) for a slice of traffic. LLM outputs are not perfectly repeatable, so if\nproduction agrees with itself on 96% of routing decisions, a candidate at 94% is only about two points\nworse than \"the same system again\", not six. Without the baseline, nondeterminism is misread as\nregression.\n\nThen convert the adjudicated sample back into traffic terms, because that is what the customer feels:\n`share of traffic made worse ≈ disagreement rate × (production-better ÷ adjudicated)`. A candidate can\nwin the disagreements overall and still make a few percent of requests worse; whether that is\nacceptable depends on *which* requests, so break the production-better cases down by severity and\nsegment before deciding.\n\n### 5. Know the blind spots\nUsers never see shadow output, so there is no click, edit or thumbs signal for the candidate. Shadow\ncannot tell you whether users would have completed their task with it. In multi-turn conversations the\nshadow sees the history produced by production, so you are evaluating one-step counterfactuals, not\nfull candidate trajectories; after the first disagreement the conversations diverge. User-outcome\nquestions need a canary or A/B test afterwards.\n\n### 6. Exit criteria\nAgree them with the customer *before* starting: minimum volume and duration (so weekly cycles are\ncovered), agreement thresholds on critical fields, the candidate-better versus production-better ratio\non adjudicated disagreements, no unexplained dry-run write calls, and latency and error budgets.","example":"An asynchronous shadow worker for a claims-triage service. Production has already answered; this runs\nthe candidate with write tools stubbed and records a comparable row.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time, json\n\nWRITE_TOOLS = {\"update_claim\", \"send_email\", \"issue_payment\"}\n\nclass DryRunTools:\n    def __init__(self, real_tools, recorded_reads):\n        self.real, self.recorded, self.intended_writes = real_tools, recorded_reads, []\n    async def call(self, name, args):\n        if name in WRITE_TOOLS:                       # never execute writes in shadow\n            self.intended_writes.append({\"tool\": name, \"args\": args})\n            return {\"status\": \"ok\", \"dry_run\": True}\n        key = json.dumps([name, args], sort_keys=True)\n        if key in self.recorded:                      # reuse prod's read result: same facts\n            return self.recorded[key]\n        return await self.real.call(name, args)       # read-only, safe to execute\n\nasync def shadow_one(event, candidate, real_tools, store, timeout_s=30):\n    tools = DryRunTools(real_tools, event[\"recorded_reads\"])\n    t0 = time.monotonic()\n    try:\n        out = await asyncio.wait_for(candidate.run(event[\"input\"], tools), timeout_s)\n        err = None\n    except Exception as e:                            # shadow failures are data, not incidents\n        out, err = None, repr(e)\n    store.write({\n        \"request_id\": event[\"request_id\"], \"tenant\": event[\"tenant\"],\n        \"prod\": event[\"prod_output\"], \"cand\": out, \"cand_error\": err,\n        \"cand_latency_s\": round(time.monotonic() - t0, 3),\n        \"cand_intended_writes\": tools.intended_writes,\n        \"prod_writes\": event[\"prod_writes\"], \"versions\": candidate.versions,\n    })\n```\n\nSuppose two weeks of shadow on one customer yields 18,000 requests (illustrative). Triage category agrees\non 94%; of 1,080 disagreements, 300 sampled and SME-adjudicated split 140 candidate-better, 90\nproduction-better, 70 equivalent. Read it in three steps:\n1. **Is the win real?** Of the 230 non-tied cases, 140 (61%) favour the candidate. A rough 95% interval\n   is ±1.96 × √(0.61 × 0.39 / 230) ≈ ±6 points, so 55–67%: clearly above 50%.\n2. **What does it cost in traffic?** 6% disagree × 90/300 production-better ≈ 1.8% of all requests get a\n   worse answer, about 320 of the 18,000. If those cluster in the \"urgent injury\" category, the net win\n   is irrelevant and the candidate needs work on that segment first.\n3. **Is it safe operationally?** Intended writes match production's on the agreed cases, and p95 latency\n   is inside budget.\n\nThat evidence is the case for moving to a 5% canary, not for a full switch.","tradeoffs":["Zero user exposure in exchange for no user-outcome signal; when task completion is the KPI, shadow must be followed by a canary or A/B test rather than replacing it.","Asynchronous forking protects production latency completely but cannot measure latency under identical load; inline forking measures it but risks contention unless quotas and pools are isolated.","Mirroring all traffic gives rare cases a chance to appear but doubles inference cost; sampling cuts cost and is fine once common cases are covered, unless the risk sits in rare inputs.","Replaying recorded tool reads keeps the comparison fair, but hides failures where the candidate would have needed a different read; execute live reads when tool choice is what changed."],"pitfalls":["Forgetting to stub a write tool; the symptom is duplicate emails, records or payments that trace back to the shadow worker, often discovered by the customer first.","Sending mirrored traffic to a model or vendor the customer has not approved; the symptom is a security review finding that the shadow pipeline was an unapproved data transfer.","Measuring string-level agreement on free text; the symptom is an alarmingly low agreement rate that says nothing about quality because paraphrases count as disagreements.","Running shadow for a few days and declaring victory; the symptom is a regression in month-end or Monday traffic patterns that the short window never contained.","Reading raw agreement without a production-versus-production baseline; the symptom is a candidate rejected for \"6% disagreement\" when a second production run would have disagreed nearly as often.","Letting the shadow call share the production rate-limit quota; the symptom is production 429 errors that begin exactly when shadow mode is switched on."],"when":"Use shadow mode before replacing the model, prompt or pipeline under a live customer, before go-live when\nthe customer's legacy process or human team can act as \"production\", and whenever the customer requires\nevidence on real traffic before exposure. Skip it for changes the regression suite fully characterises\n(a formatting fix) or when doubled cost is unaffordable and a small canary is acceptable. Measure field\nagreement, adjudicated win/loss on disagreements, intended writes, latency and errors. In interviews,\nnaming side-effect isolation and the lack of user-outcome signal shows real experience.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the async fork, dry-run tool adapters, comparison records and the disagreement sampler, and keeps shadow quotas and pools separate from production's.","architect":"Decides where the fork happens, makes every tool declare read or write so dry-run is enforceable, and defines shadow, canary and full-rollout stages.","fde":"Negotiates exit criteria and data approval with the customer, runs SME adjudication of disagreements, and presents the shadow evidence to the customer's risk or change board."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants to switch their support agent to a cheaper model. Two weeks of shadow show 97%\nagreement on ticket routing and a judged tie on replies. The product lead says, \"Ship it to everyone.\"\nWhat is missing?","a":"Shadow shows the candidate behaves like production on the inputs it saw, but not how users react:\nnobody acted on its replies, and for multi-turn chats it only answered given production's history, so\nits own trajectories were never tested. I would check that the 3% routing disagreements are not\nconcentrated in a high-severity queue, compare intended write calls such as refunds against production's,\nand confirm latency and errors. Then go to a staged canary with rollback triggers on resolution rate,\nescalations and complaints, rather than a full switch."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the most important safety requirement when shadowing an agent that can call tools?","options":["The shadow must use the same random seed as production","Write tools must run in dry-run mode so the candidate records intended actions without executing them","The shadow must run on a GPU separate from the one production uses","Shadow outputs must be shown to a small fraction of users for feedback"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without dry-run writes, the candidate would duplicate or contradict real actions such as emails or payments. Showing output to users is a canary, not shadow mode."},{"q":"Why does shadow mode not replace a canary or A/B test?","options":["Shadow traffic is too small to measure anything","Shadow mode cannot measure latency","Users never see candidate outputs, so their behaviour and task outcomes under the candidate are never observed","Shadow mode only works for classification tasks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shadow gives agreement, judged quality and operational metrics on real inputs, but user outcomes need exposure. Latency can be measured in shadow, especially with an inline fork."},{"q":"In a multi-turn chat shadowed on each turn, what exactly is being evaluated?","options":["The candidate's full conversations from start to finish","The candidate's next reply given a history produced by the production system","The production system's replies as judged by the candidate","Only the first turn, since later turns cannot be mirrored"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each shadow turn is conditioned on production's previous replies, so it is a one-step counterfactual. After the first divergence, the candidate's own trajectory is never actually played out."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/per-customer-guardrails","sources":[],"why":"Customers disagree, legitimately, about what the system may do. A bank forbids anything that reads like\ninvestment advice. A pharmaceutical company needs drug dosages discussed freely, which a generic medical\nsafety filter would block. A retailer bans mentions of competitors. A hospital needs PHI redacted in\nlogs but not in the clinician-facing answer. One customer allows the agent to issue refunds up to 50\nunits of currency, another forbids refunds altogether.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail in predictable ways. **One global policy** has to be either the union of\neveryone's restrictions (so the pharma customer's core use case is blocked) or the intersection (so the\nbank's rule is missing). **Writing each customer's rules into their system prompt** (\"never mention\ncompetitors\") feels configurable, but a prompt instruction is a request to the model, not an\nenforcement point: a user can talk the model out of it, a model upgrade can change how strictly it\nfollows it, and there is no log of when it fired. **Forking code per customer** enforces rules but turns\nevery platform change into N merges, and the forks drift until nobody knows which customer has which\nfix.\n\nPer-customer guardrails make policy data: one enforcement engine, many policies, each policy versioned,\ntested and auditable per tenant.","how":"### Layers, and why the floor only tightens\nPolicy is resolved from layers, most general first:\n1. **Platform baseline.** Non-negotiable rules: illegal content, credential leakage, cross-tenant data,\n   prompt-injection defences. No customer can relax them, because the platform's own legal and\n   reputational risk does not change per contract.\n2. **Customer layer.** Set by the customer (often through their admin console) within an allowed\n   envelope: blocked topics, PII entity types to redact, allowed languages, action limits, classifier\n   thresholds.\n3. **Deployment or use-case layer.** A customer's HR assistant and its sales assistant need different\n   rules, so policy can narrow further per deployment.\n\nMerging is monotonic toward safety: deny wins over allow, numeric limits take the minimum, allowlists\nintersect, blocklists union. A *relaxation* (the pharma customer allowing dosage discussion) is not a\nlower layer overriding a higher one; it is an explicit, platform-approved exception declared in the\nenvelope, so it can be reviewed and audited.\n\n### Resolution comes from identity, not the request\nThe tenant and deployment are taken from the authenticated context (API key, SSO token, service\nidentity), never from a field in the request body or the prompt. Otherwise a caller can choose the\nloosest policy by claiming to be another tenant.\n\n### Enforcement points\nThe resolved policy is applied wherever risk enters or leaves:\n- **Input:** topic and injection classifiers, size limits, language checks.\n- **Retrieval:** ACL and document-scope filters, so the model never sees what the user may not.\n- **Tool calls:** argument validation against per-tenant limits (refund ceiling, allowed recipients),\n  checked in the tool adapter before execution, because that is the last point you control.\n- **Output:** PII redaction, banned-term and topic checks, format validation, citation requirements.\n\n### Thresholds are per customer too\nClassifier-based guards output a score; the threshold that turns a score into a block decides the\ntrade-off between false blocks and misses. That trade-off depends on the customer's traffic and risk\nappetite, so thresholds are calibrated on each customer's labelled sample (from their SMEs), and the\nfalse-positive rate is monitored in production. A threshold copied from another customer is a guess.\n\nThe calibration procedure is ordinary operating-point selection. Score the customer's labelled sample,\nthen pick the lowest threshold that meets their stated tolerance on the side they care about most,\nusually misses: \"catch at least 95% of real investment-advice responses\". Read off the false-block rate\nthat threshold implies and show it to the customer, because that number is the cost they are accepting.\nTwo cautions. Violations are rare, so a 1,000-case sample may hold only 20 positives, and a recall\nestimate from 20 positives moves 5 points per case; enrich the sample with known violations before\ntrusting it. And the operating point drifts when the customer's traffic changes, so re-check it on fresh\nlabelled samples rather than treating it as set-and-forget.\n\n### Fail-open or fail-closed\nGuards are services that time out and fail. What happens then is itself per-customer policy: a\nregulated tenant's output check fails closed (return a safe refusal or route to a human), while a\nlow-risk internal tool may fail open with the event logged and alerted. Leaving it to whatever the\nexception handler happens to do means the guardrail is silently off during exactly the incidents when\nload is highest. Streaming output raises the same question at a finer grain: either buffer and check\nbefore release (safer, adds latency to the first visible token) or check chunk by chunk and retract,\nwhich only works for rules a partial answer cannot already violate.\n\n### Change control and audit\nPolicies are versioned config validated against a schema. A change is diffed, run against that\ncustomer's guardrail test cases (which belong in their regression suite), and rolled out in stages.\nEvery block or redaction logs the policy version and rule id, so \"why was this answer blocked?\" has an\nanswer and the customer's auditors can see the rule fired.","example":"A tighten-only merge of platform, customer and deployment layers. Relaxations are *requested* by a\nlayer but only take effect if the platform's approval registry lists them for that tenant, so the review\ngate is in the code path, not in a wiki.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Optional\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Policy:\n    blocked_topics: frozenset = frozenset()\n    redact_pii: frozenset = frozenset()\n    allowed_tools: Optional[frozenset] = None     # None = no restriction at this layer\n    max_refund: float = float(\"inf\")\n    requested_exceptions: frozenset = frozenset() # e.g. {\"dosage\"}; inert until approved\n\nAPPROVED = {\"pharma-co\": frozenset({\"dosage\"})}   # owned by platform policy, change-controlled\n\ndef merge(tenant: str, *layers: Policy) -> Policy:\n    tools = [l.allowed_tools for l in layers if l.allowed_tools is not None]\n    asked = frozenset().union(*(l.requested_exceptions for l in layers))\n    granted = asked & APPROVED.get(tenant, frozenset())          # unapproved asks are ignored\n    return Policy(\n        blocked_topics=frozenset().union(*(l.blocked_topics for l in layers)) - granted,\n        redact_pii=frozenset().union(*(l.redact_pii for l in layers)),   # blocklists union\n        allowed_tools=frozenset.intersection(*tools) if tools else None,  # allowlists intersect\n        max_refund=min(l.max_refund for l in layers),                     # limits take the min\n    )\n\nPLATFORM = Policy(blocked_topics=frozenset({\"weapons\", \"dosage\"}), redact_pii=frozenset({\"ssn\"}))\nACME     = Policy(redact_pii=frozenset({\"iban\"}), max_refund=50.0,\n                  allowed_tools=frozenset({\"lookup_order\", \"issue_refund\"}),\n                  requested_exceptions=frozenset({\"dosage\"}))   # asks, but is not approved\nACME_FAQ = Policy(allowed_tools=frozenset({\"lookup_order\"}), max_refund=100.0)\n\np = merge(\"acme\", PLATFORM, ACME, ACME_FAQ)\nprint(sorted(p.blocked_topics), p.allowed_tools, p.max_refund)\n# ['dosage', 'weapons'] frozenset({'lookup_order'}) 50.0\nprint(sorted(merge(\"pharma-co\", PLATFORM, Policy(requested_exceptions=frozenset({\"dosage\"}))).blocked_topics))\n# ['weapons']\n```\n\nRead the output against the rules. Acme asked to unblock dosage, but no approval exists, so the request\nis inert. The FAQ deployment tried to raise the refund ceiling to 100 and got 50, because limits take the\nminimum. Its tool allowlist intersected Acme's down to `lookup_order`, so a refund call is rejected in the\ntool adapter before it runs. Only the pharma tenant, with a recorded approval, has the floor relaxed, and\nonly for the one topic approved.","tradeoffs":["Per-tenant policy lets one platform serve customers with opposite rules, but every customer now has a policy to test and maintain; offer a few vetted presets when most customers want similar rules.","Enforcing in code and classifiers is reliable and auditable, at the cost of latency per check; prompt instructions are cheaper but should only shape tone, never carry a rule that must hold.","Strict thresholds reduce misses but raise false blocks, which push users to work around the tool; the balance flips toward strict where one miss is a regulatory incident.","Letting customer admins edit their own policy speeds iteration, but needs schema validation, an envelope of allowed values and staged rollout, or a typo becomes a production outage."],"pitfalls":["Implementing a customer rule only as a system-prompt instruction; the symptom is a user screenshot of the assistant doing the forbidden thing after a little persuasion.","Reading the tenant id from the request payload; the symptom is a penetration test where changing one field gives a caller another tenant's looser policy.","Copying classifier thresholds between customers; the symptom is one tenant reporting constant false blocks while another reports misses, on the same model and policy.","Leaving guard timeouts unhandled so the request proceeds unchecked; the symptom is violations that cluster in high-load windows, when the classifier service was timing out and nobody noticed.","Not logging which rule and policy version fired; the symptom is support spending hours unable to explain why a specific answer was blocked or redacted.","Letting a customer layer silently relax the platform floor; the symptom is an audit finding that a baseline protection was off for one tenant with no approval record."],"when":"Use per-customer guardrails once two customers need different rules, or once a customer's compliance\nteam hands you a written policy. Start with a platform floor and a short schema of customer-settable\nfields; resist free-form rules until you have demand. Do not build it for a single-customer deployment\nwhere one policy is the customer policy. Measure per tenant: block rate, false-block rate on reviewed\nsamples, misses found in red-teaming, and time to change a rule safely. In interviews, the key ideas are\ntighten-only merging and enforcement outside the prompt.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the policy schema, the monotonic merge, enforcement hooks at input, retrieval, tool and output, and rule-level logging with policy versions.","architect":"Defines the non-negotiable platform floor, the envelope customers may configure, and that tenant identity for policy resolution comes only from authentication.","fde":"Translates the customer's compliance documents into policy config, calibrates thresholds with their SMEs on labelled samples, and walks their auditors through the logs."},"check":{"q":"A healthcare customer wants clinicians to see unredacted patient names in answers, but your\nplatform baseline redacts names everywhere. How do you support them without weakening the platform?","a":"I would not let their layer simply override the baseline. Instead the baseline gets a declared,\nreviewable exception, \"names visible in output for authenticated clinician roles in this deployment\",\napproved by whoever owns platform policy and recorded with the contract basis. Redaction stays on in\nlogs, traces and any data leaving the deployment. The exception applies only for that tenant and role,\nresolved from the SSO identity, and a guardrail test asserting names are still redacted for other roles\nand in logs goes into their regression suite."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should a customer's \"never discuss competitors\" rule not live only in the system prompt?","options":["System prompts have a strict length limit","A prompt instruction is a request the model can be talked out of, with no enforcement or audit trail","Models ignore system prompts entirely in multi-turn conversations","Prompt instructions cannot be different per customer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prompts can be per customer, which is why the approach is tempting, but they are not enforcement. A code-level output check enforces the rule and logs every time it fires."},{"q":"The customer layer sets a refund limit of 50 and that customer's deployment layer sets 100. What limit applies?","options":["Use the deployment's value because it is the most specific layer","Use the larger value to avoid blocking legitimate refunds","Use the minimum, because merging is tighten-only","Average them to balance risk and usability"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Monotonic merging means numeric limits take the minimum (50 here) and denies win, so no layer can loosen what a more general layer set. \"Most specific wins\" is the tempting rule, but it would let any lower layer relax a higher one; relaxations must be explicit, approved exceptions."},{"q":"Where should the tenant used to resolve guardrail policy come from?","options":["A tenant field in the request body","The model's own inference from the conversation","A header that the client application sets freely","The authenticated identity, such as the API key or SSO token"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Anything the caller controls can be spoofed to select a looser policy. Binding policy to the authenticated identity makes the resolved rules as trustworthy as the authentication."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/fine-tuning-pipelines-on-client-data","sources":[],"why":"Fine-tuning on a customer's data is sometimes the right call: a classification scheme with hundreds of\nhouse-specific labels, a rigid output format, a house writing style, or distilling a large prompted\nmodel into a smaller, cheaper one for their volume. The technical part, running supervised fine-tuning\nor LoRA, is the easy part. What makes it hard is that the data belongs to someone else.\n\nConsider what the one-off notebook approach leaves behind. Nobody can say exactly which records trained\nthe model, so when the customer's legal team asks you to delete a former employee's data, you cannot\ntell whether the weights are affected. The train/test split was random by row, so near-duplicate tickets\nsit on both sides and the eval looks better than reality. Personal data was not redacted, and the model\ncan now reproduce a customer's phone number when prompted with the right prefix. The base model gets\ndeprecated by its provider a year later and nobody can reproduce the training run. And if two customers'\ndata were combined \"because more data helps\", one customer's information can surface in the other's\noutputs.\n\nEach of these is a reason the work must be an automated pipeline with explicit gates: the same inputs and\nconfig reproduce an equivalent model (bit-identical weights are rare on GPUs because of non-deterministic\nkernels, but eval results should match within noise), every artifact has lineage, and data governance is enforced in code rather than\nremembered.","how":"### 0. Decide it is worth it\nFine-tune only after a strong prompted baseline (with retrieval where knowledge is the issue) has been\nmeasured on the customer's golden set and has plateaued. Fine-tuning is good at *behaviour*: format,\nstyle, label space, and cost/latency via distillation. It is bad at *facts that change*, because facts\nbaked into weights go stale and cannot be cited; those stay in retrieval.\n\n### 1. Permission gate\nCheck the contract and data processing agreement: is training permitted, on which data, who owns the\nresulting weights or adapter, and where may training run (region, cloud, on-prem)? Record the basis in\nthe run manifest. If it is not written down, the pipeline should refuse to start.\n\n### 2. Extract and minimise\nPull only the fields the task needs, from a snapshot with a recorded identifier and time, so the run is\nreproducible and deletion requests can be checked against it.\n\n### 3. Redact and filter\nDetect and mask PII and secrets that the task does not need (names, account numbers, credentials),\nbecause a model trained on them can memorise and regurgitate them. Then deduplicate (exact and near-\nduplicate), drop low-quality or mislabelled examples, and balance classes. On customer data, cleaning\noften removes a large share of the raw records, which is normal.\n\n### 4. Split without leakage\nSplit by **group** (customer account, case, document, or time period), not by row. Tickets from one\ncase share wording; if they land on both sides, the test set measures memorisation. A time-based split\nalso matches the deployment reality: train on the past, predict the future. Grouping does not catch\neverything (the same template email can open many unrelated cases), so after splitting run a\nnear-duplicate check *across* the boundary (MinHash or embedding similarity above a high threshold) and\nmove or drop test items that have a near-twin in train. Report how many you removed; a large number\nmeans the dataset is more repetitive than it looked.\n\n### 5. Format and train\nRender examples in the base model's exact chat template, with the same system prompt the production\nsystem will use. Prefer a **per-customer adapter** (LoRA or another PEFT method) over full fine-tuning:\nadapters are small, cheap to retrain, isolate each customer's data in its own artifact, and can be\ndeleted when the contract ends. Never pool customers' data unless every customer has agreed.\n\nWhere training runs is a separate decision with its own consequences. A provider's hosted fine-tuning\nAPI is the least engineering, but the customer's data goes to that provider (which their data agreement\nmust allow), the tuned model typically lives only on that provider and cannot be exported, and when the\nprovider retires the base model you retrain on whatever replaces it. Self-hosted training on an open\nmodel keeps data in the customer's boundary and gives you a portable adapter, at the cost of owning GPUs,\nthe training stack and serving. If the training targets are outputs of a large prompted model\n(distillation), check that model's terms as well: some providers restrict using outputs to train other\nmodels.\n\n### 6. Evaluate against the baseline\nCompare the tuned model with the prompted baseline on the customer's held-out golden set and regression\nsuite, and run general capability and safety checks to catch catastrophic forgetting or loosened refusal\nbehaviour. Add **memorisation probes**: prompt with prefixes of training records and check whether\nredacted-class strings (or planted canary strings) come back.\n\nAlso train on nested subsets (say 25%, 50% and 100% of the training groups) and plot the golden-set score\nagainst size. If the curve is still climbing at 100%, more labelled data is the cheapest next gain; if it\nflattened at 50%, more data will not help and the ceiling is in the labels, the task definition or the\nbase model. This one plot answers the customer's most common question, \"should we label more?\", with\nevidence instead of opinion.\n\n### 7. Register with lineage\nRegister the artifact with the dataset snapshot hash, the record-id manifest, code version, base model\nid and version, hyperparameters, eval results and the permission record. That manifest is what lets you\nanswer \"was record X used?\" and rerun the pipeline when needed.\n\n### Deletion is retraining\nRemoving a record's influence from trained weights is not reliably possible today (machine unlearning is\nan active research area, not a guarantee). The practical answer to a deletion request is to drop the\nrecord and retrain, which is only cheap if the pipeline is automated and adapters are small. Deletion\nalso has to reach everything derived from the snapshot: cached tokenised datasets, the golden set if the\nrecord was in it, eval logs that quote it, and old adapter versions still in the registry.","example":"The leakage-safe split and the manifest step, the two parts most often done wrong. Records are grouped by\ncase, split by a stable hash of the group key, and the manifest records exactly which ids went where.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json\n\ndef bucket(key: str, salt: str = \"split-v1\") -> float:\n    h = hashlib.sha256(f\"{salt}:{key}\".encode()).hexdigest()\n    return int(h[:8], 16) / 0xFFFFFFFF            # stable in [0, 1]\n\ndef split_and_manifest(records, tenant, snapshot_id, permission_ref, test_frac=0.15):\n    assert permission_ref, \"no recorded training permission: refusing to run\"\n    assert all(r[\"tenant\"] == tenant for r in records), \"cross-tenant data in batch\"\n    train, test = [], []\n    for r in records:                               # all tickets of one case land together\n        (test if bucket(r[\"case_id\"]) < test_frac else train).append(r)\n    manifest = {\n        \"tenant\": tenant, \"snapshot_id\": snapshot_id, \"permission_ref\": permission_ref,\n        \"train_ids\": sorted(r[\"id\"] for r in train),\n        \"test_ids\": sorted(r[\"id\"] for r in test),\n        \"train_cases\": len({r[\"case_id\"] for r in train}),\n        \"test_cases\": len({r[\"case_id\"] for r in test}),\n    }\n    blob = json.dumps(manifest, sort_keys=True).encode()\n    manifest[\"manifest_sha256\"] = hashlib.sha256(blob).hexdigest()\n    return train, test, manifest\n```\n\nIllustrative scenario (numbers chosen for the walkthrough, not a benchmark): 40,000 raw support tickets\nbecome 26,000 after deduplication and quality filters. They belong to 9,000 cases, so the 15% bucket puts\nroughly 1,350 cases (around 3,900 tickets, since cases vary in size) in test. The cross-boundary\nnear-duplicate check then finds 210 test tickets with a near-twin in train, mostly auto-generated\n\"password reset\" requests, and drops them. A LoRA adapter on a mid-size open model is compared with the\nprompted large-model baseline on the customer's golden set:\n\n- Baseline: 86% label accuracy. Adapter at 25%, 50%, 100% of training cases: 81%, 85%, 87%. The curve is\n  still rising, so more labels would likely help.\n- The adapter is within noise of the baseline at a fraction of the per-request cost, general capability\n  and refusal checks are unchanged, and no canary string comes back in 500 prefix probes.\n\nThat combination, not the accuracy number alone, is what moves it to shadow mode. Had the row-split\nversion been run instead, the same adapter would have scored higher offline, because near-identical\ntickets from the same case would sit on both sides, and the gap would have surfaced only in production.","tradeoffs":["Per-customer adapters isolate data and make deletion and retraining cheap, but cap how much the model can learn compared with full fine-tuning; full tuning is worth it only with large data and ownership terms that allow it.","Aggressive redaction lowers memorisation risk but can remove signal the task needs (names in entity extraction); keep a field only when the task cannot work without it, and probe for leakage.","A fine-tuned small model is cheaper and faster than a prompted large one at high volume, but every base- model change means retraining; at low volume the prompted model's flexibility usually wins.","Group or time splits give honest numbers that look worse than random splits; accept the lower number, because it is the one production will show."],"pitfalls":["Changing the split salt or re-extracting a live table between runs; the symptom is a \"rerun\" whose test ids differ from the original manifest, so before-and-after scores are not comparable.","Splitting by row; the symptom is an excellent offline score followed by a much worse production result on new cases.","Training without a recorded permission basis; the symptom is a legal review that orders the model deleted, with no way to prove what it contained.","Formatting training data in a different template or system prompt from production; the symptom is a tuned model that does well in the training harness and inconsistently in the product.","Skipping memorisation probes; the symptom is a red-team or user finding real customer identifiers in generated text.","Mixing customers' data into one model \"for more signal\"; the symptom is one customer's terminology or records appearing in another's outputs, which is a cross-tenant leak.","Treating the run as one-off; the symptom is a deprecation notice for the base model and nobody able to reproduce the dataset or hyperparameters."],"when":"Build the pipeline when a prompted baseline has plateaued on a behaviour fine-tuning is good at, the\ncustomer permits training, and volume or latency makes a smaller tuned model pay off. Do not fine-tune to\nadd knowledge that changes, to fix retrieval failures, or before a golden set exists to prove the gain.\nMeasure the tuned model against the prompted baseline on held-out groups, plus cost per request,\nforgetting and memorisation checks. In interviews, emphasise permission, group splits, adapters for\nisolation and the fact that deletion means retraining.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements extraction, redaction, deduplication, group splits, template-exact formatting, adapter training and memorisation probes as one rerunnable pipeline.","architect":"Decides adapter-per-tenant versus shared models, where training runs relative to data residency, and the lineage records the registry must hold.","fde":"Secures the written training permission, works with customer SMEs on labels and the golden set, and explains to the customer what deletion requests mean for trained models."},"check":{"q":"Six months after you deliver a fine-tuned adapter, the customer must delete all data about one of\ntheir clients. What do you do, and what would have made it easy?","a":"First check the run manifest: if none of that client's record ids appear in the training set, the\nadapter is unaffected and you document that. If they do, I would not rely on unlearning, because removing a\nrecord's influence from weights is not dependable; I would drop the records from the snapshot, rerun the\npipeline, re-evaluate against the same golden set and replace the adapter, then delete the old one. That is\neasy only if the pipeline is automated, inputs are versioned snapshots with id manifests, and the model is\na small per-customer adapter rather than a shared multi-customer model."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should a fine-tuning dataset of support tickets be split by case rather than by individual ticket?","options":["Case-level splits produce more training examples","Tickets in the same case share content, so a row split leaks near-duplicates into the test set and inflates scores","Training frameworks require all examples from a case to be in one batch","Row-level splits make the dataset impossible to deduplicate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Related records on both sides of the split let the test measure memorisation. Grouping by case, account or time gives a score that reflects new, unseen inputs."},{"q":"What is the most dependable way to honour a request to delete records that were used for training?","options":["Run a machine unlearning algorithm on the existing weights","Add the records to a blocklist in the output guardrail","Remove the records from the dataset snapshot and retrain from the pipeline","Lower the sampling temperature so memorised text is less likely"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Unlearning methods do not yet give reliable guarantees, and output filters hide rather than remove influence. Retraining without the records is dependable when the pipeline is automated and reproducible."},{"q":"A customer's product catalogue changes weekly. What should fine-tuning on their data be used for?","options":["Teaching the model current prices and stock levels","Replacing retrieval so the model answers without documents","Nothing, since fine-tuning never helps with catalogue tasks","Teaching output format, tone or label scheme, while retrieval supplies the changing catalogue facts"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Facts in weights go stale and cannot be cited. Fine-tuning shapes stable behaviour; changing knowledge belongs in retrieval, where it can be updated and grounded."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/model-integration","sources":[],"why":"In customer work the model is often not your choice. A bank requires inference inside its own cloud\ntenancy, so you must use the models its cloud offers. A defence customer is air-gapped, so it is an\nopen-weight model on its GPUs. Another customer has already negotiated an enterprise agreement with a\ndifferent provider. And your own fine-tuning pipeline produces a new adapter every few months. Each\ntime, the same application has to run on a model it was not built against.\n\nThe naive approach is to change the endpoint and model name in config and run a smoke test. It fails in\nways a smoke test does not catch. The prompt was tuned against the old model's habits, so instructions\nthe old model followed reliably are now followed loosely. Tool calls come back in a different structure,\nor the model emits arguments as a JSON string instead of an object. The new tokenizer counts the same\ntext as more tokens, so a context budget that fit now truncates retrieved documents. A self-hosted\nmodel served with the wrong chat template produces outputs that look plausible but are measurably worse,\nwith no error anywhere. A \"max tokens\" parameter means something different. Rate-limit errors carry a\ndifferent status code, so the retry logic treats them as fatal.\n\nNone of these crash in a smoke test; they show up as quality loss on the customer's traffic weeks later.\nIntegration has to make differences explicit and measure their effect.","how":"### 1. Normalise behind an adapter\nThe application talks to one internal interface: messages in; text, tool calls, usage, finish reason\nand a typed error out. Each provider or serving stack gets an adapter that maps to and from it. Because\nthe application depends on the interface, not the vendor SDK, swapping models changes one adapter and a\nconfig entry instead of every call site. Gateways and libraries (an AI gateway, LiteLLM and similar)\ngive you much of this mapping, but you still own the contract and its tests.\n\n### 2. Declare capabilities, check them at startup\nModels differ in context window, maximum output, tool calling, parallel tool calls, schema-constrained\noutput, vision input and streaming events. Record these per model in a capability entry and have the\napplication check what it needs when it loads its config, so \"this deployment needs strict JSON schema\nand the configured model does not support it\" fails at deploy time rather than in production.\n\n### 3. Recompute budgets with the right tokenizer\nContext budgeting (how many retrieved chunks fit, when to compact history) must use the target model's\ntokenizer or token-counting endpoint. The same text can tokenize to noticeably different counts across\nmodel families, and that difference is the whole safety margin.\n\n### 4. Get the serving details right for self-hosted models\nUse the model's own chat template (from its tokenizer config), the correct stop tokens and the\nrecommended sampling defaults. Template mismatches degrade quality silently, so a template unit test that\nrenders a known conversation and compares it with the expected string is worth having.\n\n### 5. Port the prompts, do not copy them\nTreat prompts as model-specific artifacts, versioned per model in the prompt registry. Run the\ncustomer's regression suite, read the failures, adjust instructions and examples, and repeat. Expect\nformat and refusal behaviour to need the most work.\n\n### 6. Map errors and quotas\nTranslate each provider's errors into your own taxonomy (rate limited, overloaded, context too long,\ncontent filtered, invalid request, auth) so retries, fallbacks and alerts behave the same on every model.\nSize concurrency to the customer's quota on their deployment, which is often lower than yours. Map\nparameters, not just responses: an output limit can be required on one API and optional on another,\nand on reasoning models it may also have to cover hidden reasoning tokens, so a limit copied from a\nnon-reasoning model can truncate answers.\n\n### 7. Pin versions and gate the switch\nConfigure an exact model version or snapshot, not a moving alias, so the model cannot change under you.\nThen: adapter contract tests, the customer's regression suite, shadow mode on their traffic, and a\nper-tenant routing switch with the old model as fallback. Register what was deployed (model id, version,\nadapter, prompt version) so incidents can be traced to a specific combination.\n\nA **fallback model is an integrated model too**. If the router fails over from the primary to a second\nmodel during an outage, that second model needs its own capability entry, ported prompt and suite run.\nFailing over to an unported model turns an availability incident into a quality incident that is harder\nto see.","example":"Two adapters mapping raw response shapes (as plain dicts, the way they arrive over HTTP) into one\ninternal `Completion`. The two shapes differ in exactly the ways that break naive integrations: where tool\ncalls live, whether arguments are a JSON string or an object, and what the finish reasons are called.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass Completion:\n    text: str\n    tool_calls: list = field(default_factory=list)   # [(name, args_dict)]\n    finish: str = \"stop\"                             # stop | length | tool_use | filtered\n    input_tokens: int = 0\n    output_tokens: int = 0\n\ndef from_openai_chat(r: dict) -> Completion:\n    ch, msg = r[\"choices\"][0], r[\"choices\"][0][\"message\"]\n    calls = [(c[\"function\"][\"name\"], json.loads(c[\"function\"][\"arguments\"]))  # args: JSON string\n             for c in msg.get(\"tool_calls\") or []]\n    finish = {\"stop\": \"stop\", \"length\": \"length\", \"tool_calls\": \"tool_use\",\n              \"content_filter\": \"filtered\"}[ch[\"finish_reason\"]]\n    return Completion(msg.get(\"content\") or \"\", calls, finish,\n                      r[\"usage\"][\"prompt_tokens\"], r[\"usage\"][\"completion_tokens\"])\n\ndef from_anthropic_messages(r: dict) -> Completion:\n    text = \"\".join(b[\"text\"] for b in r[\"content\"] if b[\"type\"] == \"text\")\n    calls = [(b[\"name\"], dict(b[\"input\"]))                                     # args: object\n             for b in r[\"content\"] if b[\"type\"] == \"tool_use\"]\n    finish = {\"end_turn\": \"stop\", \"stop_sequence\": \"stop\", \"max_tokens\": \"length\",\n              \"tool_use\": \"tool_use\"}[r[\"stop_reason\"]]\n    return Completion(text, calls, finish,\n                      r[\"usage\"][\"input_tokens\"], r[\"usage\"][\"output_tokens\"])\n```\n\nThe application branches only on `finish == \"length\"` or `\"tool_use\"`. Without the mapping, code that\nchecks `finish_reason == \"length\"` silently never fires on a provider that says `max_tokens`, so\ntruncated answers are shipped as complete. (Field names follow these two public APIs at the time of\nwriting. Both adapters raise on an unknown finish value, which is the strict choice: providers add values\nover time, and defaulting an unknown one to \"stop\" is how truncation or refusals go unnoticed.)\n\nNow the budget arithmetic that the adapter cannot fix. A deployment packs a 32,000-token window as:\nsystem prompt 1,500 + history 4,000 + 10 retrieved chunks × 2,000 = 25,500 input tokens, plus 4,000\nreserved for output, leaving a 2,500-token margin. Suppose the new model's tokenizer counts this\ncustomer's text 15% higher (illustrative; measure it on their documents, since the ratio depends on\nlanguage and content). Input becomes about 29,300, plus 4,000 output reserved, about 33,300: over the\nwindow. Depending on the stack, requests now fail with a context-length error, or a packer that trusts the\nold counts silently drops the last two chunks. The second is worse, because it shows up only as lower\nanswer quality on long, document-heavy questions, which is exactly where the customer's hard cases are.","tradeoffs":["A normalising adapter makes models swappable, but a lowest-common-denominator interface hides features only one model has; expose such features as optional capabilities rather than dropping them.","Using a gateway or library saves mapping work, but adds a dependency whose own updates can change behaviour; pin its version and keep your contract tests, especially for tool calls and streaming.","Pinning exact model versions gives stable behaviour, but you must plan migrations before the provider retires them; aliases avoid that work only when unannounced behaviour changes are acceptable.","The customer's mandated model satisfies their data and procurement rules, but may score lower; show the measured gap on their suite so the trade-off is their informed decision, not a surprise."],"pitfalls":["Reusing the old prompt unchanged; the symptom is a sudden rise in format and instruction-following failures on the regression suite with no code change.","Counting tokens with the wrong tokenizer; the symptom is context-length errors or silently truncated retrieved documents on long requests.","Serving an open-weight model with the wrong chat template; the symptom is outputs that look fluent but score clearly below the model's expected quality, with no errors logged.","Configuring a moving alias instead of a pinned version; the symptom is a quality or format change on a date when nothing was deployed.","Configuring a fallback model that was never run against the customer's suite; the symptom is a quality dip and format failures that coincide exactly with the primary provider's outage window.","Not mapping provider errors; the symptom is retries hammering a model on non-retryable errors, or giving up immediately on rate limits."],"when":"Treat every model change as an integration project: a new provider, a customer-mandated deployment, a\nfine-tuned adapter, or a version bump of the same model. Scale the effort to the size of the change: a\nversion bump may need only the regression suite and shadow; a new model family needs the adapter,\ncapability entry, tokenizer, prompt porting and errors. Measure paired flips on the customer suite,\nformat-failure rate, latency, cost and error rates. In interviews, listing the non-obvious differences\n(templates, tokenizers, tool-call formats, error semantics) shows hands-on experience.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the adapter and its contract tests, maps errors and tool-call formats, fixes chat templates and token counting, and ports prompts against the regression suite.","architect":"Defines the internal model interface, the capability registry and per-tenant model routing, so customer-mandated models plug in without forking the application.","fde":"Gets the customer's endpoint, quotas and allowed models, runs their suite on that model, and explains measured quality differences to their stakeholders."},"check":{"q":"A customer requires you to move from your hosted provider to an open-weight model on their GPUs.\nAfter switching the endpoint, answers look fine in a demo but the regression suite drops sharply. What\ndo you check, in order?","a":"First the mechanical causes, because they are cheap and common: that the server applies the\nmodel's own chat template and stop tokens, that tool-call output is parsed correctly, and that context\nbudgets use the new tokenizer so documents are not being truncated. Then sampling settings and maximum\noutput. Only after those are clean would I attribute the remaining drop to the model, and then port the\nprompt by reading failures, and if needed consider a larger model or a fine-tuned adapter. Throughout I\ncompare paired flips against the old model on the same suite."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should context budgeting use the target model's own tokenizer?","options":["Tokenizers determine the model's sampling temperature","The same text can produce different token counts across model families, which changes what fits","Only the target tokenizer can detect prompt injection","Token counts are identical everywhere, but the API requires it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Budgets sized with another model's tokenizer can overflow the context or truncate retrieved documents. Counting with the target tokenizer keeps the safety margin real."},{"q":"A self-hosted model produces fluent but noticeably worse answers than expected, and no errors are logged. What is a likely integration cause?","options":["The GPU is too slow for the model","The new model's context window is larger than the old model's","The rate limiter is rejecting half the requests","Conversations are rendered with the wrong chat template"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The model was trained on a specific conversation format; a mismatched template still yields fluent text, so it degrades quality silently. Slow GPUs and rate limits cause latency and errors, not this."},{"q":"What is the main reason to configure a pinned model version rather than a moving alias?","options":["So the model cannot change underneath a validated deployment without a deliberate, tested switch","Pinned versions are always cheaper per token","Aliases are not allowed in customer contracts","Pinned versions have larger context windows"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A moving alias can point at a new model at any time, invalidating the evals you gated on. Pinning makes every change an explicit integration step with its own regression run."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/production-feedback-classification","sources":[],"why":"A live deployment produces far more feedback than anyone can read. Explicit signals are sparse and\nterse: a thumbs-down with no comment, or \"wrong again\". Implicit signals are plentiful but ambiguous: a\nuser regenerating an answer, heavily editing a draft, abandoning a session, or escalating to a human.\nTickets from the customer's support desk mix model failures with login problems and feature requests.\n\nThe naive approaches fail. **Tracking one satisfaction rate** gives a trend line with nothing to act on:\na rise in thumbs-down could be retrieval missing a newly uploaded policy, a guardrail over-blocking, or\nlatency. **Reading feedback ad hoc** gives vivid anecdotes and a priority list set by whoever complained\nmost memorably. **Classifying only the comment text** misattributes cause: \"this is wrong\" says nothing\nabout whether the retriever missed the document, the model ignored it, or the source document itself\nwas out of date. Those three have three different owners and three different fixes.\n\nClassification makes feedback countable and routable: how many failures of each kind, for which\ncustomer, in which component, and whether that is rising. It is the input to the regression suite (which\nfailures to capture as tests), to the product roadmap, and to the customer conversation.","how":"### 1. Build the taxonomy bottom-up\nStart with error analysis: pull a few hundred negative traces, read each, and write a free-text note on\nwhat went wrong (open coding). Group the notes into categories. This matters because categories invented\nupfront (\"hallucination\", \"bad answer\") are too vague, while categories from real traces map to fixes. A\ntypical first taxonomy for a RAG assistant: retrieval miss, ignored context, unsupported claim, wrong\nformat, over-refusal or over-blocking, outdated source, latency or timeout, out of scope, missing\ncapability (a feature request), user error, and other. Each category gets a definition, positive and\nnegative examples, and an owning component.\n\n### 2. Add severity and owner as separate fields\nCategory says what happened; severity says how bad (cosmetic, wrong but harmless, wrong and acted upon,\nsafety or compliance); owner says who fixes it (retrieval, prompt, guardrails, ingestion, product,\ncustomer content). Keeping them separate lets you sort by severity within a category and route by owner.\n\n### 3. Classify the trace, not the comment\nThe classifier gets the user input, retrieved passages, tool calls, final output, guardrail events and\nthe user's feedback. With retrieval visible it can tell \"the answer was in no retrieved passage\" (retrieval\nmiss) from \"it was in passage 2 and the answer contradicts it\" (ignored context). An LLM classifier with\na structured output schema works well here; small fine-tuned classifiers are cheaper once you have\nenough labels.\n\n### 4. Calibrate against humans, per class\nHave SMEs label a sample using the same codebook, measure their agreement first (if humans cannot agree,\nthe definition is the problem), then measure classifier precision and recall per class. Report per class\nbecause overall accuracy hides that, for instance, \"unsupported claim\" is rarely detected. Re-check after\nany taxonomy, prompt or model change.\n\n### 5. Route by confidence\nHigh-confidence labels flow straight to dashboards and owners' queues. Low-confidence or high-severity\nitems go to a human review queue, because those are where a wrong label is most costly.\n\nBe careful where \"confidence\" comes from. A number an LLM writes into its own JSON is a weak signal and\nis often poorly calibrated, so do not trust the 0.7 cut-off because it looks like a probability. Either\ncalibrate it (on the SME-labelled sample, find the score above which labels are, say, 90% correct, and\nuse that as the threshold) or derive confidence from agreement: classify each item two or three times,\nor with two different models, and send disagreements to humans. Agreement costs more tokens but is\nmeasurable.\n\n### 6. Watch for taxonomy drift\nTrack the share of \"other\" and of low-confidence items. When it rises, a new failure mode has appeared\n(a new document type, a new user population): do another round of open coding and revise the taxonomy,\nwith a version number so trends across versions are interpretable.\n\n### Know the sampling bias\nFeedback comes from users who bother to give it, skewed negative and toward heavy users. Use it to find\nand rank failure modes, then estimate their true prevalence on a random sample of traffic before claiming\n\"12% of answers have retrieval misses\".\n\nEven on a random sample, the classifier's own errors distort the rate. For one category, with recall\n(true-positive rate) *r* and false-positive rate *f* measured against SME labels, the observed share\n*p_obs* relates to the true share *p* by `p_obs = r·p + f·(1 − p)`, so `p ≈ (p_obs − f) / (r − f)`.\nIf the classifier flags 3% of a random sample as \"unsupported claim\" but only catches half of the real\nones (*r* = 0.5) with a 1% false-positive rate, the true share is about (0.03 − 0.01) / 0.49 ≈ 4%: the\ndashboard understates the category by a quarter. The correction is only as good as the *r* and *f*\nestimates, which is one more reason to keep a labelled calibration sample per customer.\n\n### Handle it as customer data\nComments and traces contain personal and confidential information. Classification runs under the same\ndata agreement as production, and stored labels inherit the trace's retention and access rules.","example":"A trace-level classifier with a fixed schema and confidence routing. `llm_json` stands for any client call\nthat returns JSON validated against the schema.\n\n```python\nfrom enum import Enum\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nclass Category(str, Enum):\n    retrieval_miss = \"retrieval_miss\"; ignored_context = \"ignored_context\"\n    unsupported_claim = \"unsupported_claim\"; wrong_format = \"wrong_format\"\n    over_refusal = \"over_refusal\"; outdated_source = \"outdated_source\"\n    missing_capability = \"missing_capability\"; user_error = \"user_error\"; other = \"other\"\n\nclass Label(BaseModel):\n    category: Category\n    severity: int = Field(ge=1, le=4)         # 1 cosmetic ... 4 safety/compliance\n    evidence: str                              # quote from the trace supporting the label\n    confidence: float = Field(ge=0, le=1)\n\nOWNER = {\"retrieval_miss\": \"retrieval\", \"ignored_context\": \"prompt\", \"unsupported_claim\": \"prompt\",\n         \"wrong_format\": \"prompt\", \"over_refusal\": \"guardrails\", \"outdated_source\": \"customer_content\",\n         \"missing_capability\": \"product\", \"user_error\": \"enablement\", \"other\": \"triage\"}\n\ndef classify(trace: dict, codebook: str, llm_json) -> dict:\n    prompt = (f\"{codebook}\\n\\nUSER INPUT:\\n{trace['input']}\\n\\nRETRIEVED:\\n\" +\n              \"\\n\".join(f\"[{i}] {p}\" for i, p in enumerate(trace[\"passages\"])) +\n              f\"\\n\\nTOOL CALLS:\\n{trace['tool_calls']}\\n\\nGUARDRAIL EVENTS:\\n{trace['guardrail_events']}\" +\n              f\"\\n\\nOUTPUT:\\n{trace['output']}\\n\\nUSER FEEDBACK:\\n{trace['feedback']}\")\n    label = Label.model_validate(llm_json(prompt, schema=Label.model_json_schema()))\n    needs_human = label.confidence < 0.7 or label.severity >= 3 or label.category == Category.other\n    return {\"trace_id\": trace[\"id\"], **label.model_dump(mode=\"json\"),\n            \"owner\": OWNER[label.category.value], \"queue\": \"human\" if needs_human else \"auto\"}\n```\n\nThe routing rule is deliberately asymmetric: severity 3 and above goes to a human whatever the\nconfidence, because a confidently wrong label on a compliance failure costs far more than a reviewer's\nminute.\n\nIllustrative week (numbers invented for the walkthrough): 1,400 negative signals for one customer. About\n150 are not model failures at all (login problems, feature requests) and route straight to product or\nsupport. Of the rest, retrieval misses and outdated sources are the largest groups, and roughly\ntwo-thirds of the outdated-source items cite one policy document the customer replaced last month.\nRanking by count alone would put that first anyway, but rank by count × severity and a smaller group of\n40 \"unsupported claim\" items about refund eligibility (severity 3: users acted on them) jumps the queue,\nand every one of them was sent to human review by the severity rule. Two fixes follow: re-ingest the\nreplaced policy (an ingestion and customer-content fix, not a model fix) and add the 40 refund cases to\nthe customer's regression suite before changing the prompt.","tradeoffs":["An LLM classifier handles free text and full traces with little setup, but costs tokens per item and drifts with model changes; a small trained classifier is cheaper once you have a few thousand labels.","A fine-grained taxonomy points directly at fixes but lowers agreement between annotators; merge categories whose distinction does not change who fixes the problem.","Auto-routing high-confidence labels saves reviewer time, but low-frequency severe failures can be mislabelled confidently; always send the top severities to humans regardless of confidence.","Implicit signals such as edits and regenerations give far more volume than thumbs, but are noisier; weight them lower and validate their meaning on a sample before using them as targets."],"pitfalls":["Classifying only the user's comment; the symptom is most items labelled \"inaccurate answer\" with no way to tell retrieval failures from generation failures.","Inventing the taxonomy upfront; the symptom is a large \"other\" bucket and categories that do not map to any team's backlog.","Reporting overall classifier accuracy only; the symptom is a dashboard where a rare but critical class never appears because the classifier almost never predicts it.","Trusting the classifier's self-reported confidence as a probability; the symptom is a human-review queue that rarely catches errors while confidently mislabelled items flow into owners' backlogs.","Treating feedback rates as prevalence; the symptom is alarming numbers that a random-sample audit of traffic does not reproduce.","Changing the taxonomy without versioning; the symptom is a trend chart with a sudden jump that is a relabelling artifact, not a real change."],"when":"Start as soon as a deployment produces more feedback than one person reads in a day; before that, read\neverything yourself and build the first taxonomy from it. Re-run open coding after major launches or new\ncustomer onboarding. Do not automate classification before humans agree on the codebook. Measure per-\nclass precision and recall against SME labels, the \"other\" share, and time from feedback to routed owner.\nIn interviews, trace-level classification and bottom-up taxonomies distinguish real practice from\ndashboard talk.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the trace-level classifier with a strict schema, the routing by confidence and severity, and per-class evaluation against human labels.","architect":"Ensures traces capture retrieval, tool and guardrail data with stable ids so feedback can be joined to them, and that labels inherit data retention rules.","fde":"Runs the open-coding sessions with customer SMEs, agrees severity definitions with the customer, and brings the weekly classified summary to the customer review."},"check":{"q":"Thumbs-down rate for one customer doubled after a release. Your classifier says most new items\nare \"unsupported claim\". How do you confirm and act?","a":"First check the classifier itself: sample the new items and have an SME label them, because a\nrelease can also change what the classifier sees. If confirmed, compare the traces: are the claims absent\nfrom retrieved passages (retrieval changed) or present but contradicted (prompt or model changed)? Diff\nthe release for retrieval, prompt and model changes, run the customer regression suite, and add the\nconfirmed failures as cases. Estimate prevalence on a random traffic sample, since feedback is biased,\nthen roll back for that tenant if the regression is real and severe."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should the classifier see retrieved passages and tool calls, not only the user's comment?","options":["Comments are too short to embed","It reduces the classifier's token cost","Cause and owner depend on whether the information was retrieved and then ignored, or never retrieved at all","Users' comments are not allowed to be stored"],"answer":2,"explanation":"\"This is wrong\" is the same comment for a retrieval miss and for ignored context, but they have different owners and fixes. Only the trace shows which one happened."},{"q":"A feedback taxonomy shows a growing share of items labelled \"other\". What does it most likely indicate?","options":["The classifier's temperature is too high","A new failure mode has appeared that the taxonomy does not cover, so open coding should be repeated","Users are giving less feedback overall","The severity scale has too many levels"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A rising \"other\" share means real items do not fit existing definitions. Reading a sample and revising the versioned taxonomy keeps categories mapped to actual fixes."},{"q":"Why is the rate of a category in feedback not a good estimate of its prevalence in all traffic?","options":["Feedback is always anonymised, so it cannot be counted","LLM classifiers cannot estimate rates","Prevalence is only defined for classification tasks","Feedback comes from a self-selected, negatively skewed group of users, so it over- or under-represents failures"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Feedback is excellent for discovering and ranking failure modes, but its sample is biased. A random traffic sample labelled with the same codebook gives the true rate."}]},{"key":"customer-data-evaluation/feedback-loops-to-product","sources":[],"why":"Forward-deployed teams sit closest to real usage. They see that the date parser fails on one\ncustomer's European formats, that the retriever cannot handle scanned tables, that users at three banks\nall want the same approval step. Under delivery pressure the rational move is a local fix: a prompt\noverride, a pre-processing script, a customer-specific branch. The customer is happy this week.\n\nRepeated across customers, that rationality turns into a mess. The same date-parsing problem is solved\nfour times, four slightly different ways, in four deployments. The product team, which owns the shared\nroadmap, never sees the pattern, because each instance was closed locally. When the platform changes,\nevery local patch is a potential break. And customers notice something else: they reported a problem,\nit was patched for them, but the next release reintroduced it, or they reported a gap and never heard\nback. After a few rounds they stop reporting, which removes the signal entirely.\n\nThe naive fix, \"FDEs should file tickets\", fails because a ticket per complaint is noise: a product\nmanager cannot weigh 300 one-line tickets against each other. What works is a deliberate loop with a\nunit of evidence a product team can act on, a cadence, decision rules, and a closing step back to the\ncustomer.","how":"### 1. The unit is an issue cluster with evidence\nClassified feedback (see production feedback classification) is grouped into clusters: one failure\nmode or request, with linked traces, counts, the customers affected, severity, the current workaround,\nand its cost (hours spent, deals blocked, workaround maintenance). A cluster lets the product team\ncompare items on the same terms, which raw tickets never do.\n\n### 2. Aggregate across customers\nThe same cluster is merged across deployments, with per-customer counts kept. This is where patterns\nappear: a problem that is a quirk for one customer is a product gap when it appears in four. Because\ncustomer data cannot be pooled freely, clusters carry redacted or synthetic examples plus links to the\ntraces held under each customer's data rules.\n\n### 3. Triage on a cadence with decision rules\nA regular (for example weekly) triage between field and product leads decides each cluster:\n- **Customer configuration:** the behaviour is customer-specific and legitimate; express it as tenant\n  config, not code.\n- **Product fix:** the cause is in shared code, prompts or models; it goes on the product backlog.\n- **Roadmap candidate:** a missing capability, ranked with the roadmap rather than squeezed in.\n- **No change:** user error or out of scope, answered with documentation or training.\nDecision rules make triage predictable, for example: anything severe is a product fix; anything seen in\nseveral customers is reviewed for productisation; a local workaround is allowed only with a tracking issue\nand an expiry.\n\nThe config-versus-product line is the decision people get wrong most. The test is whether the\nbehaviour is *correct but different* or *wrong*. \"Dates in this market are day-first\" is a legitimate,\nstable customer difference, so it is configuration. \"The parser guesses the order and is sometimes\nwrong\" is a defect, and hiding it behind a per-customer flag leaves it live for every customer who has\nnot set the flag yet.\n\n### Rank clusters on comparable terms\nTriage needs a way to compare clusters that does not reward volume of complaint. One reasonable scheme\n(a convention to adapt, not a standard) scores each cluster as:\n\n`priority = Σ over affected customers of (affected share of that customer's traffic × severity weight) + workaround cost`\n\nwith severity weights such as 1 cosmetic, 3 wrong-but-harmless, 10 acted-upon, 30 compliance, and\nworkaround cost expressed on the same scale (for example one point per engineer-day per quarter).\nUsing *share of traffic* rather than *number of tickets* is the point: a customer whose users complain\nloudly about a cosmetic issue on 20% of requests scores 0.2 × 1 = 0.2, while a silent acted-upon error on\n3% of requests at each of three customers scores 3 × 0.03 × 10 = 0.9. Whatever the exact weights, writing\nthem down makes the trade-off arguable, and strategic-account weight becomes an explicit term rather than\na side conversation.\n\n### 4. Every fix closes three things\nA resolved cluster: (a) adds regression cases to each affected customer's suite and, if general, to the\nshared suite, so it cannot silently return; (b) retires the local workarounds, which is how the deployments\nconverge back onto the product; (c) is communicated to the customers who reported it, with the release it\nshipped in. The third step is what keeps customers reporting.\n\n### 5. Measure the loop itself\nTrack time from first report to decision and to shipped fix; recurrence rate (clusters that reopen);\nnumber of open local workarounds and their age; share of field patches upstreamed; and feedback volume\nper active user over time. Falling feedback with flat usage often means people gave up, not that quality\nimproved.\n\n### Watch the loop's own failure modes\nLoud customers dominate unless clusters are weighted by breadth and severity. Optimising a single signal\nsuch as thumbs-up rate invites changes that please users without helping them (Goodhart's law). And\nusing accepted outputs as training data needs both permission and care: users often accept plausible\nbut wrong answers, so the model can drift toward its own errors.","example":"Worked scenario. Four FDE teams run a document-extraction product at four insurers. Over a quarter, the\nclassified feedback contains a cluster \"dates parsed with month and day swapped\" in three deployments.\nEach team wrote its own fix: one a prompt line (\"dates are DD/MM/YYYY\"), one a regex post-processor, one\na customer-specific parser branch. The fourth insurer has the same problem undetected, because its users\ncorrect dates by hand and never complain.\n\nAt triage, the cluster shows three customers, linked traces, a severity of \"wrong and acted upon\" (dates\ndrive claim deadlines) and roughly a week of combined engineering time spent on three workarounds\n(illustrative). The rule \"severe and seen in more than one customer\" makes it a product fix: the\nextraction schema gains a locale field in tenant config, and the parser uses it. Each of the four\ncustomers' regression suites gets day-first date cases; the shared suite gets cases for both orders.\nThe three local workarounds are deleted, one of which, the regex, had been mangling ISO dates. The fourth\ninsurer's edit-rate data, checked because the cluster prompted it, confirms the silent problem, and their\nconfig is set too. All four receive a release note naming the fix. The next quarter, the cluster stays\nclosed and the number of open workarounds goes down by three.","tradeoffs":["Upstreaming fixes removes duplicate work and patch drift, but is slower than a local fix; allow local workarounds when a customer is blocked, provided each has a tracking issue and an expiry.","Weighting clusters by breadth protects the roadmap from the loudest customer, but can starve a single strategic customer's real need; give account strategy an explicit, visible weight instead of a backdoor.","Rich clusters with traces and cost make decisions easy, but take FDE time to assemble; automate clustering from the feedback classifier so FDEs only add context and cost.","Expressing customer differences as tenant config instead of branches keeps one codebase, but each flag multiplies the combinations that must be tested; once flags interact, prefer a few vetted presets and retire flags nobody has changed in months.","Closing the loop with every reporter builds trust and keeps feedback flowing, but adds communication work; batch it into release notes per customer rather than individual replies."],"pitfalls":["Fixing locally and closing the ticket; the symptom is the same failure mode appearing as \"new\" in each new customer deployment.","Sending raw tickets to product instead of evidence clusters; the symptom is a product backlog nobody triages and field teams saying \"product never listens\".","Shipping product fixes without regression cases; the symptom is a customer reporting the same problem again after a later release.","Never retiring workarounds after the product fix; the symptom is local patches conflicting with the new behaviour, like a post-processor double-correcting fixed output.","Turning a defect into a per-customer config flag because that ships fastest; the symptom is a new customer hitting the \"already fixed\" bug on day one because nobody set their flag.","Reading falling feedback volume as rising quality; the symptom is churn or low renewal from a customer whose dashboards looked quiet."],"when":"Set up the loop once a product has more than one customer deployment or field team, and well before\nlocal workarounds become a maintenance burden. A single pilot needs only a shared list between the FDE\nand the product owner. Measure time to decision and to fix, recurrence, open workaround count and age,\nand feedback per active user. In interviews for FDE and architect roles, describing how field learning\nreaches the product, and how you avoid forking the product per customer, is a strong signal.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Upstreams fixes behind tenant config instead of customer branches, adds the regression cases, and removes the local workaround once the product fix ships.","architect":"Designs the product so customer differences are configuration, owns the triage decision rules, and tracks workaround debt as a platform risk.","fde":"Assembles evidence clusters from their deployment, argues for them in triage, and closes the loop with the customer when the fix ships."},"check":{"q":"Your FDE teams ship fast because they patch locally, and the product team complains that\ndeployments have drifted. How would you set up a loop without slowing the field teams down?","a":"Keep local patches allowed, but make each one register a tracking issue with an expiry date and\nlink it to a feedback cluster. Clusters are built automatically from classified feedback across customers,\nand a weekly triage with field and product leads applies explicit rules: severe or multi-customer clusters\nbecome product fixes, customer-specific behaviour becomes tenant config. Each product fix must add\nregression cases and delete the matching workarounds. I would track open workaround count and age and time\nto upstream, and send customers release notes when their issue ships so they keep reporting."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the most useful unit for passing field feedback to a product team?","options":["One ticket per user complaint, filed as soon as it arrives","A cluster per failure mode or request with traces, affected customers, severity and workaround cost","A monthly average satisfaction score per customer","A list of the customers who complained the most"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A cluster lets the product team compare items on breadth, severity and cost. Raw tickets are too noisy to weigh, and averages or complaint counts say nothing about what to fix."},{"q":"After a product fix ships for a cross-customer problem, what should happen to the local workarounds?","options":["Keep them in place as a backup in case the fix regresses","Leave them until the customer asks for removal","Remove them, with regression cases ensuring the product fix covers each customer","Convert them into the default behaviour for new customers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Leftover workarounds diverge and can conflict with the fix, for example double-correcting outputs. Regression cases, not stale patches, are the right guard against the problem returning."},{"q":"Feedback volume per active user has fallen steadily while usage is flat. What is a plausible and worrying interpretation?","options":["The feedback classifier has become more accurate","The model has certainly improved, so users have less to report","Usage analytics are miscounting sessions","Users have stopped reporting because earlier reports produced no visible change"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Falling feedback can mean better quality, but it also matches users giving up on a loop that never closes. Check with a random traffic sample and ask whether reporters heard back."}]}]});
